{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/palette.candidate-b.report.md reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/palette.adopt-b.report.md'","aggregated_output":"# Decision 0003: the default categorical palette\n\nStatus: **adopted by the owner on 2026-11-18.** The palette below is the one the owner chose, candidate B (commit 987988b), and it is the 1.0 default.\n\nHow it got here:\n1. **First version.** Commit 51e1be5 was reviewed by Codex gpt-6.2-astra (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/palette.review.md`). The review confirmed the arithmetic and showed four things wrong:\n   - the uneven lightness was claimed to be forced, and it is not;\n   - simulated screenshots and 8-bit fills fell below the limits the record promised;\n   - the order explorer could prune a feasible order after a timeout;\n   - the record overclaimed in several places.\n2. **Refit.** The refit (52bbdf0) added byte-level checks and corrected the text. It was reviewed again (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/misc-opus.review.md`, part B; reported to me as Codex gpt-6.2-sol). That review found a more even light scheme.\n3. **Candidate B.** I refitted around that scheme by eye. The owner chose candidate B over the refit, which is kept below as \"Superseded\".\n\nThe hard constraints are measured and tested. Nobody other than me has reviewed candidate B.\n\nSpec: A5 (palette thresholds, provisional), L2 (3:1 for marks), F9 (eight series). Finding R7, docs/01-landscape.md.\n\n## The palette\n\nThe slot order is blue, orange, green, purple, red, cyan, magenta, slate. That is the first version's order, and hue families are kept per slot.\n\n| Slot | Family | Light | OKLCH | Contrast on #ffffff | Dark | OKLCH | Contrast on #16181d | Hue shift |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | blue | `#2b97ee` | 0.66 0.160 248 | 3.10 | `#5d9afd` | 0.69 0.160 260 | 6.35 | 12° |\n| 2 | orange | `#cf8020` | 0.67 0.140 65 | 3.11 | `#dc772f` | 0.67 0.150 52 | 5.69 | 13° |\n| 3 | green | `#0b6c49` | 0.47 0.100 162 | 6.45 | `#ade4ba` | 0.87 0.081 152 | 12.33 | 10° |\n| 4 | purple | `#812cc4` | 0.50 0.220 305 | 6.79 | `#9443a9` | 0.53 0.171 319 | 3.07 | 14° |\n| 5 | red | `#be1506` | 0.51 0.200 30 | 6.36 | `#ba4f1f` | 0.56 0.150 42 | 3.57 | 12° |\n| 6 | cyan | `#0380a8` | 0.56 0.110 229 | 4.51 | `#38c7fe` | 0.78 0.140 229 | 9.11 | 0° |\n| 7 | magenta | `#8c1382` | 0.45 0.190 332 | 8.30 | `#d991b8` | 0.74 0.100 346 | 7.35 | 14° |\n| 8 | slate | `#8e948f` | 0.66 0.010 151 | 3.10 | `#6d736e` | 0.55 0.011 151 | 3.66 | 0° |\n\n`configs/fit-final.json` reproduces these arrays. Both exact searches complete with cost 0, so the run is deterministic: `node research/palette/fit.mjs --config=research/palette/configs/fit-final.json --seconds=300`. `palette.ts` holds the arrays it writes.\n\n**One token differs from the first version.** The dark focus token is a near-white `#e3e8f0` (14.4:1 on #16181d), not `#79a8ff`. The refit made that change, and candidate B changes no token.\n- **Why the change.** The old focus blue sits at OKLab L 0.735, exactly where a dark-mode series blue belongs. With it kept, no dark set I found had a first blue above L 0.55. The exact search with the blue asked for at 0.62 ended at `#3970c2`, L 0.55 (`configs/dark-n2.json`, best within 300 s).\n- **Against today's palette.** `#79a8ff` is 0.050 from today's dark blue `#5d9afd`, under constraint 5's 0.10.\n- **Light focus.** `#1f5fcc` is unchanged; `keyboard.browser.test.ts` hardcodes it.\n\n**Before and after.**\n\n| | 51e1be5 | Refit 52bbdf0 (superseded) | Now (candidate B) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Light OKLab L, lowest to highest | 0.39 to 0.68 | 0.43 to 0.68 | 0.45 to 0.67 |\n| Light, spread across the first three | 0.26 | 0.25 | 0.20 |\n| Light, largest chroma | 0.17 | 0.205 (purple) | 0.220 (purple) |\n| Eighth's chroma, light / dark | not recorded here | 0.026 / 0.031 | 0.010 / 0.011 |\n| Dark first blue | `#1c73c5`, L 0.55, 3.64:1 | `#64acfa`, L 0.73, 7.46:1 | `#5d9afd`, L 0.69, 6.35:1 |\n| Dark, largest chroma | 0.15 | 0.177 (purple) | 0.171 (purple) |\n| Largest light/dark hue shift | 18.5° (slate), 8° chromatic | 13.6° (red) | 14.1° (purple) |\n| Byte-rounded simulation, worst pair | 0.098 (light protan 6–8) | 0.102 | 0.102 |\n| 25% fills on an 8-bit raster, worst neighbours | 0.0590 (review's raster) | 0.063 (Chrome) | 0.064 light, 0.063 dark (Chrome) |\n\n**What B buys:**\n- a light set with less spread;\n- an ochre instead of a hot orange in slot 2;\n- a lighter slot-3 green;\n- a grey eighth in both schemes.\n\n**What B costs:**\n- a louder light red and purple;\n- a dimmer dark purple;\n- tighter dark margins to the focus and muted tokens;\n- a deeper dark first blue.\n\n\"How B was made and why it was chosen\" gives the measurements of both lists.\n\n![Light scheme preview](../../research/palette/preview-light.png)\n\n![Dark scheme preview](../../research/palette/preview-dark.png)\n\nEach preview shows:\n- the swatches, with hex, contrast and nearest neighbour;\n- lines with 3, 5 and 8 series at 2 px, with Axylo's end markers;\n- three overlapping areas at 25%;\n- eight stacked areas at 80%;\n- an eight-slice donut;\n- grouped bars.\n\nThe same charts are repeated under the three simulations (the Machado matrices as SVG color matrices in linear RGB) and in grayscale. Source: `research/palette/preview.html`.\n\n## What is measured, and on what\n\nThere are three forms of measurement:\n\n- **Analytic.** The 8-bit hex is decoded, then simulated or composited in floating point, then converted to OKLab.\n- **Bytes.** Values are rounded where an 8-bit image would round them. A simulated color is encoded and rounded to the nearest byte before the OKLab conversion. A translucent fill is quantised as a raster would quantise it: alpha at round(α×255), each channel rounded. That reproduces Chrome 157's canvas and SVG fills on white exactly and is within one byte on #16181d. A second variant puts alpha at floor(α×255), which is what the review's CPU Skia raster did.\n  - **The rounding model is not the preview image.** Chrome's SVG filter can differ from the model by a byte. For the shipped palette, every sampled donut pixel of the preview equals the model. For the superseded palette one did not: in light tritanopia, slot 7 was `#b30f41` in the PNG and `#b20f41` in the model, which took the light tritan 5–7 distance from 0.102 in the model to 0.1005 in the image.\n- **Chrome.** Two records come from Chrome itself:\n  - the fill pixels Chrome actually drew, canvas and SVG (`research/palette/raster.mjs`);\n  - the preview PNG itself: `preview.mjs` reads back the middle of every donut slice in every row of the image it just wrote (`preview-pixels.json`).\n\nThe unit test requires the analytic and the byte forms. It also checks:\n- the recorded Chrome fill pixels against the 0.06 limit;\n- the recorded preview pixels against 0.10;\n- that each recorded pixel is within one byte of its model.\n\nA stricter margin is the worst case over every byte color within one byte per channel of both fills. It is recorded but not required. The shipped light scheme meets it (0.060); the dark scheme does not (0.057).\n\n## Constraints, measured\n\nThese values come from `research/palette/palette.json`, which `score.mjs` writes. The unit test recomputes each value with its own code and compares it at the written precision, including which pair is closest. The fields it checks are listed in `research/palette/README.md`. Limits are applied to unrounded values, and the record keeps three decimals.\n\n| Constraint | Check | Limit | Light | Dark |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | lowest contrast on background | ≥ 3 | 3.10 (slate) | 3.07 (purple) |\n| 2 | min pairwise ΔE, all 8, normal / protan / deutan / tritan | ≥ 0.10 | 0.113 / 0.102 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.115 / 0.105 / 0.102 / 0.103 |\n| 2, bytes | the same after the simulation is rounded to bytes | ≥ 0.10 | 0.113 / 0.103 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.115 / 0.105 / 0.102 / 0.103 |\n| 2, image | the same on the donut slices read back from the preview PNG | ≥ 0.10 | 0.113 / 0.103 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.115 / 0.105 / 0.102 / 0.103 |\n| 3 | first 4, worst vision (analytic; bytes) | ≥ 0.14 | 0.144 tritan; 0.143 | 0.162 deutan; 0.164 |\n| 3 | first 3, 5, 6, worst vision (analytic) | ≥ 0.10 | 0.149 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.188 / 0.113 / 0.105 |\n| 4 | largest light/dark OKLCH hue shift | ≤ 30° | 14.1° (purple) | same pairs |\n| 5 | nearest series color to focus / reference / muted | ≥ 0.10 | 0.116 (cyan) / 0.121 (green) / 0.111 (cyan) | 0.104 (green) / 0.107 (green) / 0.102 (pink) |\n| 6 | neighbour fills at 25%: ideal / byte models / Chrome canvas / Chrome SVG | ≥ 0.06 | 0.065 / 0.064 / 0.064 / 0.064 | 0.064 / 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 |\n| 6 | neighbour fills at 80%: ideal / byte models / Chrome | ≥ 0.06 | 0.223 / 0.224 / 0.224 | 0.175 / 0.174 / 0.175 |\n| 6, recorded only | 25% fills, worst case within one byte | (0.06) | 0.060 | 0.057 |\n\n\"Muted\" is my addition to constraint 5: the donut draws its \"Other\" slice in the muted token, next to series slices.\n\n### Prefix minima (constraint 3)\n\nEach cell is analytic / bytes, with the closest slots.\n\n| Prefix | Vision | Light | Dark |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| first 3 | normal | 0.263 / 0.263 (1–3) | 0.268 / 0.268 (1–3) |\n| first 3 | protan | 0.149 / 0.149 (2–3) | 0.259 / 0.257 (1–3) |\n| first 3 | deutan | 0.235 / 0.235 (2–3) | 0.208 / 0.208 (2–3) |\n| first 3 | tritan | 0.206 / 0.205 (1–3) | 0.188 / 0.186 (1–3) |\n| first 4 | normal | 0.247 / 0.247 | 0.229 / 0.229 |\n| first 4 | protan | 0.149 / 0.149 | 0.224 / 0.226 |\n| first 4 | deutan | 0.154 / 0.153 | 0.162 / 0.164 |\n| first 4 | tritan | 0.144 / 0.143 | 0.182 / 0.182 |\n| first 5 | normal / protan / deutan / tritan | 0.197 / 0.106 / 0.102 / 0.144 | 0.115 / 0.122 / 0.113 / 0.113 |\n| first 6 | normal / protan / deutan / tritan | 0.119 / 0.106 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.115 / 0.120 / 0.107 / 0.105 |\n\n`node research/palette/score.mjs --md` prints the full tables, with pair identities for every row.\n\n### Tight pairs and margins\n\nThese are every measured value within 5% of its limit (`palette.json`, `tight`):\n\n- **Light:**\n  - contrast 3.096 (slate; the solver keeps 3.02);\n  - all-eight pairwise 0.102–0.103 under the three deficiencies, analytic and in bytes;\n  - first four 0.144 under tritanopia (0.143 in bytes);\n  - first five and six 0.102–0.103.\n- **Dark:**\n  - contrast 3.074 (purple);\n  - all-eight pairwise 0.102–0.105;\n  - first six 0.104 in bytes;\n  - focus distance 0.104 (green against `#e3e8f0`);\n  - muted distance 0.102 (pink against `#9da2ad`).\n- **Fills, both schemes:** the 25% neighbours have about 0.003–0.005 of room; the 80% fills have a lot.\n\nHow fragile is that to a hand edit? I tried every one-byte change of one channel of one color. 44 of 48 light edits and 48 of 48 dark edits still meet every required check (`palette.json`, `oneByteEdits`). A changed hex still fails the record test until `score.mjs` is rerun.\n\nThe solver kept margins above the tested limits: pairwise 0.102, fills 0.063, contrast 3.02, and a hue shift of 15° (`configs/fit-final.json`, `solverLimits`). B is not at the edge of its boxes: with fills at 0.065 and pairwise at 0.103, the same boxes still hold solutions that differ by a few bytes in several colors. I kept B as chosen and did not search for the largest margins.\n\n## Focus token under a two-tone ring\n\nThe second review (part B, finding 1) showed that a single-ink focus indicator cannot work with these colors:\n- **Dark.** `#e3e8f0` is under 3:1 against five dark series colors: blue 2.27, orange 2.54, green 1.17, cyan 1.58 and pink 1.96. Purple (4.70), red (4.04) and slate (3.94) pass. No single ink reaches 3:1 against both `#16181d` and the light green; even white reaches only 1.44:1 against it.\n- **Light.** The same holds: `#1f5fcc` is between 1.08 and 1.90:1 against every light series color.\n\nA datum outline crossing a mark therefore fails WCAG 1.4.11 wherever it is drawn in one ink. The chart core is changing the indicator to two tones: a ring in the focus token, with a surround in the background color between the ring and the marks. That work is not in my files.\n\n**What the token must satisfy under the two-tone ring:**\n\n1. **The focus color must reach 3:1 against the background.** The ring is seen against its surround, which is the background color. Light `#1f5fcc` on `#ffffff` is 5.89:1; dark `#e3e8f0` on `#16181d` is 14.43:1. Both pass, and the L2 token test asserts it.\n2. **The surround must reach 3:1 against every mark it touches.** That is constraint 1 read the other way: every series color reaches 3:1 on its background (lowest 3.10 light, 3.07 dark), and so do the axis and reference tokens. Nothing new is needed.\n3. **The focus color needs no contrast against series colors.** The surround keeps them from touching.\n\n**Constraint 5 is no longer needed for contrast.** That constraint is the 0.10 OKLab distance between every series color and the focus token, and it never could provide contrast: an OKLab distance is not a contrast ratio. What it buys is identity. The ring's color never coincides with a series color, so a focused datum's outline is not read as a mark of its own or another series. That matters most for slot 1, the most-used series. The old dark focus `#79a8ff` is 0.050 from today's dark blue `#5d9afd`, so a ring around a series-1 point would be drawn in nearly that series' own color. I recommend keeping constraint 5. Dropping it would allow `#79a8ff` to return (7.49:1 on the background, so it would pass under two tones) at the cost of that confusion. This is the owner's call.\n\n## How B was made and why it was chosen\n\n**Where it started.** It started from the second review's pair (part B, \"More even light scheme\"):\n\n```text\nlight  #1e97fa #c7872b #0f6f48 #8636c1 #c61405 #4f81b7 #921184 #8c9695\ndark   #64acfa #fbab2d #96e8c6 #9f46ae #fe573f #387bf0 #d571aa #5f6866\n```\n\nThat pair meets every limit, and I confirmed it with the same code. By eye, it has two faults:\n- **Its sixth slot is a second blue.** Light `#4f81b7` is 2° of hue from `#1e97fa`, and dark `#387bf0` is 9° from `#64acfa`.\n- **Its dark red shouts.** `#fe573f` (chroma 0.207 at L 0.68) is the loudest mark in the dark scheme.\n\nSo I did three things:\n- moved slot 6 to cyan and teal hues (`configs/b-cyan.json`, `b-cyan-45.json`);\n- held both eighths at chroma 0.015 or less;\n- held the dark red and orange at chroma 0.15 or less.\n\n**How the sets were found.**\n- **Light.** It came from annealing, then an exact local search (`configs/b-polish-light.json`).\n- **Dark.** It came from an exact search against the light set (`b-polish-dark.json`).\n- **Reproduction.** `configs/candidate-b.json` puts small boxes around both with the solver margins. Its slots are the same as today's `fit-final.json`, and it is kept as the record of the candidate.\n\n**Against the superseded palette and the review's pair:**\n\n| | Superseded | Review's pair | B (shipped) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Light L, lowest to highest | 0.43–0.68 | 0.46–0.67 | 0.45–0.67 |\n| Light L spread, first three | 0.25 | 0.19 | 0.20 |\n| Light largest chroma | 0.205 (purple) | 0.207 (purple, red) | 0.220 (purple) |\n| Hue gap, slot 1 to slot 6, light / dark | 20° / 27° | 2° / 9° | 19° / 30° |\n| Eighth's chroma, light / dark | 0.026 / 0.031 (hue 283 / 274) | 0.012 / 0.011 | 0.010 / 0.011 |\n| Dark largest chroma | 0.177 (purple) | 0.207 (red) | 0.171 (purple) |\n| Dark first blue | `#64acfa`, L 0.73, 7.46:1 | `#64acfa` | `#5d9afd`, L 0.69, 6.35:1 |\n| Lowest contrast, light / dark | 3.03 / 3.26 | 3.03 / 3.09 | 3.10 / 3.07 |\n\n**What B costs, as measured:**\n- **A louder light red and purple.**\n  - `#be1506` is a full red (chroma 0.20), like the review's `#c61405`. It is the loudest line in the light \"8 lines\" panel, and the superseded coral was not.\n  - The light purple is at chroma 0.220, above both other sets. In B's neighbourhood, a cap of 0.207 is proved infeasible: the exact search in `configs/b-purple-207.json` completes with no solution.\n  - Calming the red pushes purple up instead. Red at 0.18 or 0.17 is feasible only with purple at 0.23 (`b-red-18.json`, `b-red-17.json`), and red at 0.16 is proved infeasible (`b-red-16.json`).\n- **A dark purple at 3.07:1,** against 3.26 in the superseded set.\n- **Tighter dark margins to the tokens:** focus 0.104 and muted 0.102. The superseded set had 0.138 and 0.113.\n- **A dark first blue at 6.35:1** (`#5d9afd`, L 0.69) instead of `#64acfa` (7.46:1). Keeping `#64acfa` against this light set was not found (`b-dark-blue.json`): the exact search timed out and annealing found nothing.\n- **A light floor at 0.45, not the review's 0.46.** With every light color at 0.46 or above, my searches found only a steel-blue sixth (`b-walk3.json`, hue 242) or nothing (`b-cyan.json`, `b-indigo.json`). That is a search result, not a proof.\n- **A more saturated dark cyan.** `#38c7fe` has chroma 0.14, against 0.11 in the superseded set. I asked for 0.115; the exact search went to 0.14.\n\n**Why it was chosen.** The owner chose B over the superseded palette for its more even light scheme, a calmer first three and a neutral eighth. The comparison images the owner chose from are kept as they were: `research/palette/compare-light.png` and `compare-dark.png`, superseded above and B below, with the same seven panels in normal vision. What can be checked in them:\n- **\"3 lines\", light.** In the superseded set, the deep green line is clearly the heaviest and the orange the hottest. In B, the ochre and the lighter green sit closer to the blue.\n- **Swatch 8, both images.** The superseded slate reads as a lavender grey; B's reads as grey in both schemes.\n- **Swatches 1 and 6, and the donut.** Both sets show sky blue against teal in light and blue against cyan in dark.\n- **\"8 lines\" and the donut, dark.** B's pink is dusty rather than candy-bright, and its red is a brick.\n\n**The candidate's own records.** These are `palette-b.json`, `raster-b.json`, `strokes-b.json`, `strokes-b-raw.json.gz`, `preview-b-*.png` and `preview-b-pixels.json`, all written before adoption. I regenerated the shipped records from `palette.ts` in Chrome 157. The result is identical to the candidate's records byte for byte: the raster, stroke and raw stroke records, the preview pixels and the light PNG. `palette.json` equals `palette-b.json` except for the latter's `candidate` field.\n\n## Thin lines\n\n`strokes.mjs` draws each color as a 2 px random-walk polyline in headless Chrome 157 and reads the pixels back. It draws through canvas and SVG, at device pixel ratios 1 and 2. For each pixel column it records two values:\n\n- **Core:** the most covered pixel.\n- **Weighted:** the coverage-weighted mean of the touched pixels, bg + (Σc²/Σc)(ink − bg), with coverage at or below 0.02 dropped.\n\nThis is a legibility measurement of this rendering path, not the WCAG test. WCAG 1.4.11 allows the specified stroke color to be assessed despite antialiasing, and A5 is met on the swatch.\n\nWeighted p10 / median contrast, by renderer and device pixel ratio (`strokes.json`):\n\n| Scheme, slot | Color | Swatch | Canvas DPR 1 | SVG DPR 1 | Canvas DPR 2 | SVG DPR 2 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| light 1 | `#2b97ee` | 3.10 | 2.50 / 2.59 | 2.48 / 2.59 | 2.77 / 2.84 | 2.77 / 2.84 |\n| light 2 | `#cf8020` | 3.11 | 2.47 / 2.56 | 2.47 / 2.56 | 2.74 / 2.82 | 2.74 / 2.82 |\n| light 3 | `#0b6c49` | 6.45 | 4.33 / 4.60 | 4.28 / 4.60 | 5.18 / 5.41 | 5.18 / 5.41 |\n| light 4 | `#812cc4` | 6.79 | 4.64 / 4.98 | 4.64 / 4.98 | 5.59 / 5.82 | 5.59 / 5.82 |\n| light 5 | `#be1506` | 6.36 | 4.86 / 5.12 | 4.86 / 5.12 | 5.63 / 5.77 | 5.59 / 5.77 |\n| light 6 | `#0380a8` | 4.51 | 3.32 / 3.49 | 3.32 / 3.49 | 3.82 / 3.97 | 3.82 / 3.97 |\n| light 7 | `#8c1382` | 8.30 | 5.70 / 6.07 | 5.62 / 6.07 | 6.88 / 7.16 | 6.82 / 7.16 |\n| light 8 | `#8e948f` | 3.10 | 2.42 / 2.50 | 2.42 / 2.50 | 2.70 / 2.79 | 2.70 / 2.79 |\n| dark 1 | `#5d9afd` | 6.35 | 4.59 / 4.84 | 4.57 / 4.83 | 5.38 / 5.58 | 5.37 / 5.58 |\n| dark 2 | `#dc772f` | 5.69 | 4.13 / 4.38 | 4.13 / 4.38 | 4.80 / 4.97 | 4.80 / 4.97 |\n| dark 3 | `#ade4ba` | 12.33 | 8.50 / 9.04 | 8.41 / 9.04 | 10.20 / 10.60 | 10.20 / 10.60 |\n| dark 4 | `#9443a9` | 3.07 | 2.43 / 2.52 | 2.43 / 2.52 | 2.72 / 2.80 | 2.72 / 2.80 |\n| dark 5 | `#ba4f1f` | 3.57 | 2.76 / 2.87 | 2.74 / 2.87 | 3.11 / 3.22 | 3.09 / 3.22 |\n| dark 6 | `#38c7fe` | 9.11 | 6.37 / 6.75 | 6.37 / 6.75 | 7.54 / 7.87 | 7.54 / 7.87 |\n| dark 7 | `#d991b8` | 7.35 | 5.24 / 5.56 | 5.24 / 5.56 | 6.17 / 6.38 | 6.17 / 6.38 |\n| dark 8 | `#6d736e` | 3.66 | 2.85 / 2.94 | 2.81 / 2.94 | 3.21 / 3.31 | 3.21 / 3.31 |\n\nWhat else the record shows:\n- **Core.** The core p10 equals the swatch to two decimals in every row and renderer.\n- **Coverage.** Counted from the raw pixel record (`strokes-raw.json.gz`), every color has a fully covered pixel in 575 of 576 columns at DPR 1 (one column without, in both renderers). At DPR 2 it has one in all 1,152.\n- **Renderers.** Canvas and SVG differ by up to 0.09 (dark green, DPR 1 p10: 8.50 canvas, 8.41 SVG), and most often by 0.00–0.05.\n- **Tested.** The unit test recomputes every statistic in this table, and both column counts, from the raw record.\n\nEvery series also has a full-color marker at its last point and at sparse intervals (spec A5).\n\n## Superseded: the refit of 52bbdf0\n\nThis palette shipped from the refit until the owner adopted B on 2026-11-18:\n\n```text\nlight  #4a94eb #e97200 #136102 #7314a2 #bd585e #007baa #a52265 #9293a5\ndark   #64acfa #de7b2e #b2e390 #9149b9 #c3513d #71d8ff #f898b5 #7a7f93\n```\n\n**Why it was replaced: uneven lightness in the light scheme.**\n- **Spread.** Light L ran from 0.43 to 0.68. Three colors (green, purple and magenta, at L 0.43–0.49) were much darker than the three near 3:1.\n- **Weight.** In the 3-series chart, the green line was clearly the heaviest of the first three (L 0.43 against 0.66 and 0.68).\n- **What B gives instead.** B has a calmer first three (ochre for a hot orange, a lighter green) and a neutral eighth. The superseded slate `#9293a5` was a lavender grey at chroma 0.026, hue 283.\n\n**Its measurements, from commit 987988b's `research/palette/palette.json`.** Pairwise cells are normal / protan / deutan / tritan.\n- **Lowest contrast:** 3.03 light (slate), 3.26 dark (purple).\n- **All-eight minimum:** 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 light; 0.116 / 0.104 / 0.103 / 0.102 dark.\n- **Fills:** the 25% neighbours were 0.063 in Chrome, and 0.056 at the worst case within one byte.\n- **One-byte edits:** 41 of 46 light and 46 of 47 dark passed.\n- **Strokes:** the lowest weighted p10 at DPR 1 was 2.38 light and 2.55 dark.\n\n**Its record.** That commit holds its Chrome records and preview PNGs. `configs/fit-superseded.json` reproduces its arrays; I ran it again here and both searches completed. It came out of a four-step refit, and an explorer fix shipped alongside:\n\n1. **Wider boxes, exact search.** I ran `fit.mjs` with light floors at L 0.44–0.48 and chroma up to 0.18–0.22 (`configs/even-a.json`, `even-c.json`). No palette was found within 300 s. That is a timeout, not a proof.\n2. **Annealing.** `anneal.mjs` searches the same grids by minimising the total shortfall. I checked it first on a known-feasible neighbourhood, where it recovered the first review's palette.\n   - From random starts it found feasible light sets only once the boxes were split into tiers: blue, orange and slate near 3:1, red and cyan in the middle, green, purple and magenta lower (`configs/tier-44-free.json`).\n   - It found nothing with every color at L 0.46 or above (`configs/tier-46-cr.json`).\n3. **Dark against that light set.** The searches with the old focus token failed: `dark-a2.json` (annealing) and `dark-n2.json` (exact; best blue L 0.55). Moving the dark focus to near-white let annealing find a set with a light dark-mode blue and a deeper purple (`configs/dark-c.json`).\n4. **Polish.** I put small boxes around both results, with the solver margins (`configs/fit-superseded.json`, formerly `fit-final.json`). In those boxes, 0.065/0.103 and 0.066/0.102 were proved infeasible.\n\nExplorer fix: `explore.mjs orders` keeps three outcomes per order: feasible, proved infeasible, timed out. It prunes only on a completed proof and prints all three. The first version's family and order statistics came from the faulty explorer. I did not rerun them in full, so they are withdrawn.\n\n## Why lightness spreads on white (corrected)\n\nThe first version said that each simulation \"removes one chromatic axis\", and that three deep colors were therefore forced. Both statements were wrong.\n\nThe Machado matrices act in linear RGB, not in OKLab. In OKLab they mostly compress the red–green direction (protanopia, deuteranopia) or the blue–yellow direction (tritanopia), but they also move lightness. For example, the first version's light blue has L 0.659 unsimulated and between 0.643 and 0.691 across the simulations. The compression is a design heuristic, not a proof.\n\nThe first review then exhibited a two-scheme palette meeting all six constraints with light L from 0.48 to 0.69:\n\n```text\nlight  #3791ff #d28204 #2b6d48 #881bbd #c70f09 #1f7fae #fe1ce0 #959396\ndark   #63d2fc #f9c309 #78aa6f #bf37be #e42129 #4574fa #ee5ec3 #7e7c80\n```\n\nIt lay outside the boxes my what-if runs had searched. That pair pays in chroma: its magenta has chroma 0.30, its red 0.21 and its purple 0.23, and one hue shift sits at 29.97°. The second review then found a pair with light L 0.460–0.673 and chroma at most 0.207. So the uneven lightness of the superseded set was not the price of restrained chroma either. B starts from that pair.\n\n## Tried and failed\n\nEach line below means \"not found\" in the searches for the superseded refit; B's own searches are listed in its section above. Only searches that complete prove anything, and those are marked.\n\n- **A lighter, calmer slot-3 green** (L 0.46 or above, chroma 0.11 or below), with restrained chroma elsewhere (`configs/green-a.json`; `green-b.json`, which also allowed a saturated red): nothing at the time.\n  - The shortfalls were green against red under protanopia, and magenta against cyan and slate.\n  - B later found one (`#0b6c49`, L 0.47, chroma 0.10), with wider hue boxes and a purple at chroma 0.22.\n- **Walking from the first review's palette toward lower chroma** while staying feasible (`configs/walk-b.json`): the green and red calmed down, but the magenta stayed at chroma 0.28 or more (`#fd26e0`).\n- **The first review's tier structure with chroma held at 0.22 or less** (`configs/cx-a.json`): nothing; magenta was always the shortfall.\n- **Red third**, as in Tableau 10 (`configs/red3-a.json`): nothing; red was short against green under protanopia.\n- **Magenta fourth instead of purple**, to relieve the dark blue (`configs/mag4-a.json`): feasible on white. Its dark set was proved infeasible with the old focus token (exact search, `configs/dark-n2-m4.json`), and annealing found none with the new one (`configs/dark-b2-m4.json`).\n- **Fills at the worst case within one byte per channel, in both schemes**: never met together with the other goals. B meets it in light (0.060) but not in dark (0.057).\n- **A palette with every light color at L 0.48 or above that also keeps chroma at 0.22 or less**: none found. My conclusion at the time was that the lightness-for-chroma trade was real. That was wrong: the second review found an L 0.46 floor at chroma 0.207 by searching a wider range of hues. My searches were limited in time, boxes and hue ranges.\n\n## Additions beyond the six constraints\n\n- **Muted token** in constraint 5 (tested). The donut's \"Other\" slice.\n- **Byte-level checks** (tested): byte-rounded simulation; fills on both 8-bit raster models; Chrome's drawn pixels within one byte of the model and 0.06 apart; the preview PNG's donut pixels 0.10 apart.\n- **Ring closure and deficiency-simulated fills** (recorded only).\n  - At 25% the donut's closing pairs go down to 0.029 (light 6–1) and 0.037 (dark 6–1).\n  - Neighbours under simulation go down to 0.028 (light, deuteranopia, 6–7).\n  - Slices are opaque in Axylo, so constraint 2 covers them.\n- **Solver margins** as listed above. The tests use the decision's limits.\n\n## For the owner\n\n- **The light red and purple are the loudest light colors** (chroma 0.20 and 0.22). Calming either costs the other (\"What B costs\").\n- **The dark focus ring is near-white `#e3e8f0`** (light stays blue).\n  - This is a visible UI change, made to free the dark blue.\n  - With the two-tone ring it meets its contrast need (14.4:1 on the background).\n  - Reverting to `#79a8ff` is possible under two tones only if constraint 5 is dropped: it is 0.050 from the dark blue (see \"Focus token under a two-tone ring\").\n- **The dark set mixes three very light colors with two dimmer ones.** The light ones are the green (L 0.87), cyan (0.78) and pink (0.74). The dimmer ones are a deep purple (0.53) and a brick red (0.56), the dimmest at 3.07 and 3.57:1.\n- **Slot hues move within each family between schemes.** The largest shift is 14.1° (purple), under constraint 4's 30°.\n\n## Reference palettes (measured)\n\nThese are read from the installed d3-scale-chromatic 3.1.1 (`research/harness/node_modules`) by `research/palette/references.mjs` and scored with the same code (`research/palette/references.json`). Minima are over the first eight colors.\n\n| Scheme | Below 3:1 on white | Min ΔE normal / protan / deutan / tritan (closest slots) |\n|---|---|---|\n| schemeTableau10 | 5 of 10 | 0.129 / 0.016 / 0.007 (3–5, red–green) / 0.064 |\n| schemeObservable10 | 6 of 10 | 0.113 / 0.059 / 0.020 / 0.046 |\n| schemeCategory10 (D3's default, Plotly's) | 4 of 10 | 0.113 / 0.007 (2–3) / 0.039 / 0.042 |\n| schemeDark2 | 2 of 8 | 0.099 / 0.018 / 0.017 / 0.049 |\n| schemeSet1 | 5 of 9 | 0.130 / 0.031 / 0.035 / 0.066 |\n\nLibrary defaults as rendered are in R7 (docs/01-landscape.md). That table has eight displayed six-slice rows. Four rows need a note:\n- Recharts' row uses its documentation's authored colors.\n- The D3 chart is authored.\n- uPlot has no default palette.\n- ECharts was read from its options.\n\nWhat I took from these:\n- the blue-then-orange opening (Tableau 10, Category 10);\n- a quiet neutral last;\n- the evidence that the familiar sets put adjacent hues that deuteranopia merges (Tableau 10's red and green at 0.007).\n\nThe first version also drew on palettes recalled from memory (IBM Carbon, Okabe–Ito, Paul Tol). No arrays for them are installed here, so they are not cited.\n\n## Not verified\n\n- **Browser suites.** The suites that sample palette colors were not run, because they need `npm run build`.\n  - `render.browser.test.ts` and `theme.browser.test.ts` read the colors from `palette.ts`, so they should follow; that is inferred.\n  - `keyboard.browser.test.ts` checks the light focus only, and that token is unchanged.\n  - `tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts` reads the light first series color from the packed stylesheet, so it should need no change; that is inferred, not run.\n- **The built bundle.** `packages/axylo/dist` (untracked) still declares the superseded colors until the next build, for example `--axylo-default-series-1: light-dark(#4a94eb, #64acfa)`.\n- **The focus ring.** The two-tone focus ring is being built in the chart core. Its rendered contrast against marks has not been measured here.\n- **Overlapping fills.** Raster checks cover isolated fills over the background. A 25% area over another 25% area is not scored.\n- **Simulation and platform.** The simulation is Machado at severity 1.0 only. Strokes and fills were measured in headless Chrome 157 on Linux only.\n- **People.** Nobody with a color-vision deficiency has looked at the previews.\n- **Review.** Nobody other than me has reviewed B. The owner chose it from the comparison images.\n\n## Files\n\n- `packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts`: both series sets, written from `candidates/fit-final.json`, and the tokens. Exported names and shapes are unchanged.\n- `packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts`: what it checks:\n  - constraints 1–6, analytic and in bytes;\n  - Chrome fill pixels;\n  - the record at its written precision;\n  - stroke-record validation;\n  - the preview PNG's pixels;\n  - the one-byte-edit count.\n- `research/palette/`: the scripts and records.\n  - Scripts: `color.mjs`, `fit.mjs`, `anneal.mjs`, `explore.mjs`, `score.mjs`, `raster.mjs`, `strokes.mjs`, `preview.mjs`, `references.mjs`.\n  - Configs: `configs/` (`fit-final.json` is the shipped palette, `fit-superseded.json` the previous one).\n  - Records: `palette.json`, `raster.json`, `strokes.json`, `strokes-raw.json.gz`, `preview-pixels.json`, `references.json`.\n  - Previews: `preview.html` and the two PNGs.\n- Record of the choice, in `research/palette/`:\n  - the comparison: `compare.html`, `compare-light.png`, `compare-dark.png`;\n  - the candidate as written before adoption: `configs/candidate-b.json`, `configs/b-*.json`, `candidates/candidate-b.json`, and the `*-b*` records.\n\n  The README lists what each file is and how to reproduce it.\n<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort xhigh), Agent-tool subagent abc842fd0288bc675, resumed. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Report: candidate B, the focus token, and findings 2–4\n\nCandidate B is built, measured and written up in decision 0003 as awaiting the owner's choice. `palette.ts` series are unchanged; its only diff is the source comment from finding 4. Nothing is committed.\n\n### 1. Candidate B\n```\nlight  #2b97ee #cf8020 #0b6c49 #812cc4 #be1506 #0380a8 #8c1382 #8e948f\ndark   #5d9afd #dc772f #ade4ba #9443a9 #ba4f1f #38c7fe #d991b8 #6d736e\n```\nIt keeps the shipped backgrounds and every token.\n\n**How it differs from the reviewer's pair:**\n- Slot 6 moved from a second blue to cyan/teal. The reviewer's slot 6 was 2° of hue from slot 1 in light and 9° in dark. B's is 19° light and 30° dark.\n- Both eighths are neutral: chroma 0.010 light, 0.011 dark.\n- Dark red and orange are held at chroma 0.15 or less. The reviewer's dark red `#fe573f` was C 0.207.\n\n**Reproduction:** `research/palette/configs/candidate-b.json` uses small boxes with the solver margins. Both exact searches complete with cost 0 and reproduce `candidates/candidate-b.json` (ran). The searches I cite are saved as `configs/b-*.json`, each with its outcome in its note.\n\n**Measured (ran).** Record: `research/palette/palette-b.json`, written by `score.mjs --palette=` from B's own Chrome records. Nothing was refused. Pairwise cells are normal / protan / deutan / tritan.\n\n| Check | Light | Dark |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. lowest contrast | 3.10 (slate) | 3.07 (purple) |\n| 2. all 8, analytic | .113/.102/.102/.102 | .115/.105/.102/.103 |\n| 2. all 8, bytes | .113/.103/.102/.102 | .115/.105/.102/.103 |\n| 2. all 8, preview PNG pixels | .113/.103/.102/.102 | .115/.105/.102/.103 |\n| 3. first 4, worst vision (analytic; bytes) | .144 tritan; .143 | .162 deutan; .164 |\n| 3. first 3 / 5 / 6, worst vision | .149 / .102 / .102 | .188 / .113 / .105 |\n| 4. largest hue shift | 14.1° | 14.1° |\n| 5. focus / reference / muted | .116 / .121 / .111 | .104 / .107 / .102 |\n| 6. 25% fills: ideal / byte models / Chrome canvas / Chrome SVG | .065/.064/.064/.064 | .064/.064/.063/.063 |\n| 6. 80% fills: ideal / byte models / Chrome | .223/.224/.224 | .175/.174/.175 |\n| 25% fills, worst within one byte (recorded only) | .060 | .057 |\n\n- **One-byte edits that still meet every check:** 44/48 light, 48/48 dark.\n- **Strokes:** lowest weighted p10 at DPR 1 is 2.42 light and 2.43 dark (shipped: 2.38 and 2.55).\n\n**Images:**\n- `research/palette/compare-light.png` and `compare-dark.png`: the same seven panels, shipped above and B below, normal vision.\n- `research/palette/preview-b-light.png` and `preview-b-dark.png`: B under all five vision rows.\n\nI looked at all four images.\n\n**Recommendation: adopt B; the owner decides.** Reasons you can check in the compare images:\n- **Calmer first three in light (\"3 lines\").** Ochre instead of a hot orange, and a lighter green.\n- **A neutral swatch 8 in both schemes.** The shipped one reads lavender-grey (C 0.026/0.031, hue 283/274).\n- **Slot 6 is clearly a teal or cyan** against the slot-1 blue.\n- **No dark colour dominates.** The pink is dusty and the red is brick.\n\n**What B costs, and what I searched:**\n- **Louder light red and purple.** Red `#be1506` (C 0.20) is the loudest light line, and purple is at C 0.220. Capping purple at 0.207 is proved infeasible near B (`b-purple-207.json`, exact search completes with no solution). Red at 0.18 or 0.17 forces purple to 0.23 (`b-red-18.json`, `b-red-17.json`); red at 0.16 is proved infeasible (`b-red-16.json`).\n- **Light floor at 0.45, not 0.46.** At 0.46 I found only a steel-blue sixth or nothing (`b-walk3.json`, `b-cyan.json`, `b-indigo.json`). That is a search result, not a proof.\n- **Dark blue is `#5d9afd` (L 0.69), not `#64acfa`.** Keeping `#64acfa` was not found: the exact search timed out and annealing over 16 seeds found nothing (`b-dark-blue.json`).\n- **Dimmer dark purple and tighter dark margins.** Dark purple is 3.07:1. Dark focus distance is 0.104 and muted 0.102.\n- **Dark cyan at C 0.14.** I asked for 0.115.\n\n**Tooling added for this:**\n- `--palette=` on `raster.mjs` and `strokes.mjs`, and `--palette/--raster/--preview/--strokes/--strokes-raw/--out` on `score.mjs`. A candidate run refuses to start without `--out`, so the shipped records can't be overwritten. I ran the default `score.mjs` again and `palette.json` came out byte-identical.\n- The README now has candidate-B reproduction steps. I also fixed its shipped reproduce order: `preview.mjs` before `score.mjs`.\n\n### 2. Focus token (finding 1)\nThis is the decision section \"Focus token under a two-tone ring\":\n- **What it must satisfy:** under a two-tone ring, the focus color must reach 3:1 against the background. Light `#1f5fcc` is 5.89:1 and dark `#e3e8f0` is 14.43:1; the L2 test asserts it.\n- **The surround needs nothing new:** it is background-colored, so its 3:1 against marks is constraint 1 (B: 3.10 / 3.07).\n- **Focus against series:** no contrast is needed. The section lists the measured single-ink failures.\n- **Is constraint 5 still needed?** Not for contrast. It buys identity: the old `#79a8ff` is 0.024 from the dark blue. I recommend keeping it; that is the owner's call.\n- **Candidate B changes no token.**\n\n### 3. Findings 2–4 (done before this phase, tests ran)\n- **Finding 2:**\n  - The decision now separates the rounding model from the preview image: light tritan slot 7 is `#b30f41` in the image and `#b20f41` in the model, 0.1005 against 0.102. There is a new \"2, image\" row.\n  - `preview.mjs --shot` writes `preview-pixels.json`, and `score.mjs` folds it in as check `2i`.\n  - Test: `${name}: the preview PNG itself (donut slices read back from each row) matches the palette and keeps 0.10 apart`.\n- **Finding 3:**\n  - The record test now covers tokens, lightness and largest chroma, all-vision composites with ring closures, and both raster models.\n  - Stroke statistics and coverage counts are recomputed from the new raw record `strokes-raw.json.gz`.\n  - The one-byte-edit count is recounted.\n  - `score.mjs` applies limits before rounding.\n  - The README lists exactly which fields are verified.\n  - Tests:\n    - `${name}: the research record holds these colors, its tokens, and the measured fields listed in research/palette/README.md at their written precision`\n    - `${name}: the 2 px stroke record is these colors, and every statistic in it follows from the raw pixel record`\n    - `${name}: the one-byte-edit count in the record is what every check gives for every one-byte edit`\n    - `${name}: 6. the same on an 8-bit raster, with alpha quantised by rounding and by flooring`\n  - Mutations that each failed a test: false stroke 1.01/1.02, token, protan composite, lightness, edit count, preview min, coverage count.\n- **Finding 4:**\n  - The stroke table is labelled by renderer: 4 renderers × 16 rows.\n  - Coverage counts come from the raw record: 575 of 576 columns at DPR 1, 1152 of 1152 at DPR 2.\n  - The canvas/SVG gap is stated as up to 0.09 (dark green, 8.32 against 8.23).\n  - The dark first-three pair is corrected to 2–3 at 0.251.\n  - The `palette.ts` comment now says \"bounded searches … (a search limit, not a proof)\".\n- **New candidate-B tests:**\n  - `meets every required check of constraints 1–6, analytic and in bytes, against the shipped tokens`\n  - `the fills Chrome drew for it (research/palette/raster-b.json) are its colors and stay 0.06 apart`\n  - `its preview PNG (…, donut slices read back) keeps 0.10 apart in every row`\n  - Two mutations of a B color made them fail (ran), and I restored the file.\n\n### Theme test run (last lines)\n```\n Test Files  3 passed (3)\n      Tests  58 passed (58)\n```\n`palette.test.ts` alone: 42 passed. `npx tsc -p packages/axylo --noEmit` printed nothing.\n\n### Not verified\n- Nobody independent has reviewed candidate B.\n- B has not been through the browser suites (they read `palette.ts` and need `npm run build`, which I did not run).\n- Chrome was headless Chrome 157 on Linux only.\n- The two-tone ring is not built or measured here; it belongs to core.\n\n### Files\n- **Decision and source:** `docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md` (new section \"Candidate B (awaiting the owner's choice)\", status, owner, not-verified and files lists) and `packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts` (comment only).\n- **Test:** `packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts` (new candidate-B block).\n- **Scripts:** `research/palette/{score,raster,strokes}.mjs` and `README.md`.\n- **New candidate-B files in `research/palette/`:**\n  - `configs/candidate-b.json` and `configs/b-*.json`\n  - `candidates/candidate-b.json`\n  - `palette-b.json`, `raster-b.json`, `strokes-b.json`, `strokes-b-raw.json.gz`\n  - `preview-b.html`, `preview-b-light.png`, `preview-b-dark.png`, `preview-b-pixels.json`\n  - `compare.html`, `compare-light.png`, `compare-dark.png`\n\nSearch scratch is under `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-canarit--claude-spares-spare-6ClT4i/23402a37-81bb-469c-a14a-8bf7f048d066/scratchpad/candb/`, not needed to reproduce.\n<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort xhigh), Agent-tool subagent abc842fd0288bc675, resumed. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Report: candidate B is now the shipped palette\n\nB now ships. `palette.ts` holds its arrays, written by a script from the regenerated `candidates/fit-final.json`, not typed in. Every default research run now describes B. The theme tests pass (57/57), and a second default `score.mjs` run reproduces `palette.json` byte for byte. Nothing is committed. `palette.ts` is still at `packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts`; I re-read it just before writing.\n\n### Diff summary (my files only)\n- **`packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts`:**\n  - the two series arrays, now `#2b97ee #cf8020 #0b6c49 #812cc4 #be1506 #0380a8 #8c1382 #8e948f` and `#5d9afd #dc772f #ade4ba #9443a9 #ba4f1f #38c7fe #d991b8 #6d736e`;\n  - two comment lines saying these are candidate B, chosen on 2026-11-18;\n  - tokens unchanged.\n- **`packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts`:** the candidate-B block is removed (−54 lines). The shipped block reads `palette.ts`, so it now asserts B. No historical block was needed.\n- **Configs:**\n  - `research/palette/configs/fit-final.json` now holds B's boxes, with the same slots as `candidate-b.json`.\n  - The old config is kept as the new `configs/fit-superseded.json`.\n  - `candidates/fit-final.json` (B) and the new `candidates/fit-superseded.json` were regenerated. Both exact searches complete, and the superseded config still reproduces the old arrays.\n- **Regenerated by the default runs:** `raster.json`, `strokes.json`, `strokes-raw.json.gz`, `preview.html`, `preview-light.png`, `preview-dark.png`, `preview-pixels.json`, `palette.json`. The fresh Chrome output is byte-identical to the candidate records written before adoption: raster, strokes, raw strokes, preview pixels and the light PNG. `palette.json` equals `palette-b.json` apart from its `candidate` field.\n- **`research/palette/README.md`:**\n  - the configs and record-of-choice rows;\n  - the reproduce block;\n  - a worked example for scoring another palette;\n  - how the comparison images were made and how to redraw them.\n\n  I ran that example on the superseded palette, and it reproduces the `palette.json` committed in 987988b exactly.\n- **`docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md`, rewritten:**\n  - status \"adopted by the owner on 2026-11-18\";\n  - B's table as the palette's table;\n  - a \"Before and after\" table across 51e1be5, the superseded refit and B;\n  - every measured table for B: constraints, prefixes, tight pairs, the focus section, strokes;\n  - a section on how B was made and why it was chosen, with the costs you listed, as measured;\n  - a \"Superseded: the refit of 52bbdf0\" section with its arrays, why it was replaced, its key numbers and how it was made;\n  - updated \"Tried and failed\", \"For the owner\", \"Not verified\" and \"Files\".\n\n  I also corrected one earlier \"not found\": B does have the calmer green (`#0b6c49`, L 0.47, C 0.10) that the earlier green-a search did not find.\n- **Comparison images:** `compare-light.png`, `compare-dark.png` and `compare.html` are untouched.\n\n### Measured table for the shipped palette (B), from the regenerated records\nPairwise cells are normal / protan / deutan / tritan.\n\n| Check | Light | Dark |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. lowest contrast | 3.10 (slate) | 3.07 (purple) |\n| 2. all 8, analytic | .113/.102/.102/.102 | .115/.105/.102/.103 |\n| 2. all 8, bytes | .113/.103/.102/.102 | .115/.105/.102/.103 |\n| 2. all 8, preview PNG pixels | .113/.103/.102/.102 | .115/.105/.102/.103 |\n| 3. first 4, worst vision (analytic; bytes) | .144 tritan; .143 | .162 deutan; .164 |\n| 3. first 3 / 5 / 6 | .149 / .102 / .102 | .188 / .113 / .105 |\n| 4. largest hue shift | 14.1° (purple) | 14.1° (purple) |\n| 5. focus / reference / muted | .116 / .121 / .111 | .104 / .107 / .102 |\n| 6. 25%: ideal / byte models / Chrome canvas / Chrome SVG | .065/.064/.064/.064 | .064/.064/.063/.063 |\n| 6. 80%: ideal / byte models / Chrome | .223/.224/.224 | .175/.174/.175 |\n| 25% worst within one byte (recorded only) | .060 | .057 |\n\n- **Record:** nothing refused, all hard constraints met.\n- **One-byte edits:** 44/48 light, 48/48 dark still meet every check.\n- **Strokes:** lowest weighted p10 at DPR 1 is 2.42 light, 2.43 dark.\n- **Preview image:** every sampled donut pixel equals the byte-rounding model. The one-byte discrepancy the review found was in the old palette's image.\n- **Solver margins:** B is not at the edge of its boxes. Probe runs found solutions at fills 0.065 / pairwise 0.103 that differ by a few bytes. I kept B exactly as chosen, and the decision says so.\n\n### Byte-for-byte check\n`node research/palette/score.mjs` printed \"all hard constraints met\". `cmp` against the previous `palette.json` showed no difference.\n\n### Theme test run (last lines)\n```\n Test Files  4 passed (4)\n      Tests  57 passed (57)\n```\n- `palette.test.ts` alone: 36 passed.\n- The fourth file is another agent's new `minify.test.ts`.\n- `npx tsc -p packages/axylo --noEmit` printed nothing.\n\n### What still refers to the old arrays\nI searched the whole repository for `#4a94eb` and `#64acfa`, and for all sixteen old colors in source, tests and docs outside the decision. Nothing in `packages/` or `tests/` hardcodes them.\n\nWhat remains:\n- **Intended references:**\n  - `docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md`: the Superseded section and history;\n  - `research/palette/candidates/fit-superseded.json`;\n  - `research/palette/configs/b-dark-blue.json`: a what-if that kept `#64acfa`;\n  - `research/palette/compare.html` and `preview-b.html`: the record of the choice.\n- **Not mine:** `reviews/2026-11-18/**` and another agent's `.scratch-treeshake/**`.\n- **Build output:** `packages/axylo/dist/axylo.css` (untracked) still declares `--axylo-default-series-1: light-dark(#4a94eb, #64acfa)` until the next `npm run build`, which I did not run.\n- **`tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts`:** it reads the light first series color from the packed stylesheet with a regex, compares it to the computed `--axylo-default-series-1`, and counts painted pixels of that color. It hardcodes no color, so it should follow the next build without change. That is inferred; I did not run it.\n\n### Not done / for you\n- **Candidate-B records.** `palette-b.json`, `raster-b.json`, `strokes-b.json`, `strokes-b-raw.json.gz`, `preview-b-*` and `candidates/candidate-b.json` are now identical to the shipped records. I kept them as the candidate's records as written before adoption; the README and decision say so. Removing them only takes deleting the files and fixing the two docs that list them — say if you want that.\n- **Browser suites** were not run (they need a build).\n- **Review:** nobody independent has reviewed B.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts'","aggregated_output":"/**\n * Default colors (decision 0003, docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md). Each scheme has eight series colors\n * in slot order: blue, orange, green, purple, red, cyan, magenta, slate. Slot k is the same hue family in both\n * schemes. packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts measures the decision's constraints: 3:1 on the scheme's\n * background; at least 0.10 apart in OKLab under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and\n * tritanopia, 0.14 among the first four; clear of the focus, reference and muted tokens; neighbours still apart\n * as 25% and 80% fills; the distances and fills also after rounding to bytes, as an 8-bit image holds them. The\n * sets were fitted by research/palette/fit.mjs (configs/fit-final.json) and are scored in\n * research/palette/palette.json. They are the decision's candidate B, which the owner chose on 2026-11-18 over\n * the previous sets for a more even light scheme, a calmer first three and a neutral eighth.\n *\n * The dark focus token is a near-white rather than a blue so that the first dark series can be an ordinary\n * dark-mode blue: with the blue focus #79a8ff kept, the bounded searches recorded in decision 0003 found no dark\n * set whose first blue was lighter than OKLab L 0.55 (a search limit, not a proof). Under the two-tone focus ring,\n * the focus color must reach 3:1 against the background and the background-colored surround carries the contrast\n * against marks (decision 0003, \"Focus token under a two-tone ring\").\n *\n * Token colors meet spec L2 on their scheme's background: axis and focus at least 3:1, muted and reference at\n * least 4.5:1 because they are also used for text (hidden legend entries, reference labels). Grid is exempt.\n *\n * Text has no default: the chart inherits the page's `color` (spec DX6).\n */\nexport interface SchemeColors {\n  readonly background: string;\n  readonly axis: string;\n  readonly grid: string;\n  readonly muted: string;\n  readonly reference: string;\n  readonly focus: string;\n  readonly series: readonly string[];\n}\n\nexport const LIGHT: SchemeColors = {\n  background: '#ffffff',\n  axis: '#5f6470',\n  grid: '#e2e4e9',\n  muted: '#62666f',\n  reference: '#474b55',\n  focus: '#1f5fcc',\n  series: ['#2b97ee', '#cf8020', '#0b6c49', '#812cc4', '#be1506', '#0380a8', '#8c1382', '#8e948f'],\n};\n\nexport const DARK: SchemeColors = {\n  background: '#16181d',\n  axis: '#8b909c',\n  grid: '#30343c',\n  muted: '#9da2ad',\n  reference: '#bcc0c9',\n  focus: '#e3e8f0',\n  series: ['#5d9afd', '#dc772f', '#ade4ba', '#9443a9', '#ba4f1f', '#38c7fe', '#d991b8', '#6d736e'],\n};\n\nexport const SERIES_COUNT = 8;\n/**\n * The default palette (spec A5, L2; decision 0003). Constraints 1–6 of the decision, measured here with code\n * of this file's own (WCAG 2 contrast; OKLab; Machado et al. 2009 color-vision simulation at severity 1.0 in\n * linear RGB, clamped, as research/harness/palette.mjs scored the ten libraries), each in two forms: analytic, on\n * unrounded values, and quantised, on the bytes an 8-bit image holds (simulated colors rounded to bytes; fills\n * through an 8-bit raster model, at the worst case within one byte per channel, and as Chrome drew them).\n * Then the record the research scorer wrote (research/palette/palette.json) is compared field by field at the\n * precision it was written in, and its browser-measured parts (strokes, Chrome fill pixels) are validated against\n * the shipped colors.\n */\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\nimport fs from 'node:fs';\nimport path from 'node:path';\nimport { gunzipSync } from 'node:zlib';\nimport { contrastRatio, parseColor, type Rgba } from '../../src/theme/color';\nimport { DARK, LIGHT } from '../../src/theme/palette';\nimport { AXYLO_CSS } from '../../src/theme/css';\n\ntype Vec3 = readonly [number, number, number];\ntype Vision = 'normal' | 'protan' | 'deutan' | 'tritan';\n\nconst c = (s: string): Rgba => parseColor(s)!;\nconst lin = (v: number): number => (v <= 0.04045 ? v / 12.92 : ((v + 0.055) / 1.055) ** 2.4);\nconst enc = (v: number): number => (v <= 0.0031308 ? 12.92 * v : 1.055 * v ** (1 / 2.4) - 0.055);\nconst clamp01 = (v: number): number => Math.min(1, Math.max(0, v));\nconst srgb = (hex: string): Vec3 => { const p = c(hex); return [p.r, p.g, p.b]; };\nconst bytesOf = (hex: string): Vec3 => srgb(hex).map(v => Math.round(v * 255)) as unknown as Vec3;\nconst vec = (f: (i: 0 | 1 | 2) => number): Vec3 => [f(0), f(1), f(2)];\n\nfunction oklab([r, g, b]: Vec3): Vec3 {\n  const l = Math.cbrt(0.4122214708 * r + 0.5363325363 * g + 0.0514459929 * b);\n  const m = Math.cbrt(0.2119034982 * r + 0.6806995451 * g + 0.1073969566 * b);\n  const s = Math.cbrt(0.0883024619 * r + 0.2817188376 * g + 0.6299787005 * b);\n  return [0.2104542553 * l + 0.793617785 * m - 0.0040720468 * s,\n    1.9779984951 * l - 2.428592205 * m + 0.4505937099 * s,\n    0.0259040371 * l + 0.7827717662 * m - 0.808675766 * s];\n}\n\nconst MACHADO: Record<Vision, readonly Vec3[]> = {\n  normal: [[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]],\n  protan: [[0.152286, 1.052583, -0.204868], [0.114503, 0.786281, 0.099216], [-0.003882, -0.048116, 1.051998]],\n  deutan: [[0.367322, 0.860646, -0.227968], [0.280085, 0.672501, 0.047413], [-0.011820, 0.042940, 0.968881]],\n  tritan: [[1.255528, -0.076749, -0.178779], [-0.078411, 0.930809, 0.147602], [0.004733, 0.691367, 0.303900]],\n};\nconst VISIONS = Object.keys(MACHADO) as Vision[];\n\n/** Linear RGB of a gamma-encoded sRGB color as seen with `vision`, clamped. */\nconst simulated = (rgb: Vec3, vision: Vision): Vec3 => {\n  const l = vec(i => lin(rgb[i]));\n  return vec(i => clamp01(MACHADO[vision][i]![0] * l[0] + MACHADO[vision][i]![1] * l[1] + MACHADO[vision][i]![2] * l[2]));\n};\n/** OKLab of a color as seen with `vision`, unrounded. */\nconst seen = (rgb: Vec3, vision: Vision): Vec3 => oklab(simulated(rgb, vision));\n/** OKLab of a byte color. */\nconst labOfBytes = (b: Vec3): Vec3 => oklab(vec(i => lin(b[i] / 255)));\n/** OKLab of the simulated color after encoding and rounding to bytes, as a simulated 8-bit image holds it. */\nconst seenBytes = (hex: string, vision: Vision): Vec3 => labOfBytes(vec(i => Math.round(255 * enc(simulated(srgb(hex), vision)[i]))));\nconst delta = (p: Vec3, q: Vec3): number => Math.hypot(p[0] - q[0], p[1] - q[1], p[2] - q[2]);\nconst oklch = (hex: string): Vec3 => { const [L, a, b] = seen(srgb(hex), 'normal'); return [L, Math.hypot(a, b), (Math.atan2(b, a) * 180 / Math.PI + 360) % 360]; };\nconst hueDifference = (a: number, b: number): number => { const d = Math.abs(a - b) % 360; return d > 180 ? 360 - d : d; };\n/** Source-over at `alpha` in gamma-encoded sRGB, as the browser composites a translucent fill, unrounded. */\nconst over = (hex: string, alpha: number, bg: string): Vec3 => { const f = srgb(hex), b = srgb(bg); return vec(i => f[i] * alpha + b[i] * (1 - alpha)); };\n/** The same fill on an 8-bit raster: alpha quantised to round(alpha × 255) (or floor, as another rasterizer did), channels rounded. */\nconst raster = (hex: string, alpha: number, bg: string, quantise: (x: number) => number = Math.round): Vec3 => {\n  const k = quantise(alpha * 255), f = bytesOf(hex), b = bytesOf(bg);\n  return vec(i => Math.round((f[i] * k + b[i] * (255 - k)) / 255));\n};\n/** Smallest difference between any byte colors within one byte per channel of `a` and of `b`. */\nfunction worstWithinOneByte(a: Vec3, b: Vec3): number {\n  const box = (p: Vec3): Vec3[] => {\n    const out: Vec3[] = [];\n    for (const i of [-1, 0, 1]) for (const j of [-1, 0, 1]) for (const k of [-1, 0, 1]) out.push(labOfBytes(vec(n => Math.min(255, Math.max(0, p[n] + [i, j, k][n]!)))));\n    return out;\n  };\n  const pa = box(a), pb = box(b);\n  let m = Infinity;\n  for (const p of pa) for (const q of pb) m = Math.min(m, delta(p, q));\n  return m;\n}\nconst hexBytes = (hex: string): Vec3 => bytesOf(hex);\n\n/** Smallest pairwise difference among `colors` under `vision`, with the slots (1-based) of the pair. */\nfunction closest(colors: readonly string[], vision: Vision, labOf: (hex: string, v: Vision) => Vec3 = (h, v) => seen(srgb(h), v)): { d: number; pair: [number, number] } {\n  const labs = colors.map(h => labOf(h, vision));\n  let d = Infinity, pair: [number, number] = [0, 0];\n  for (let i = 0; i < labs.length; i++) for (let j = i + 1; j < labs.length; j++) {\n    const x = delta(labs[i]!, labs[j]!);\n    if (x < d) { d = x; pair = [i + 1, j + 1]; }\n  }\n  return { d, pair };\n}\n\n/** Equal at the precision the record was written in (rounded to `digits` decimals). */\nconst atPrecision = (measured: number, recorded: number, digits: number): boolean => Math.abs(measured - recorded) <= 0.5 * 10 ** -digits + 1e-9;\n\ninterface PairRecord { min: number; pair: number[] }\ninterface StrokeStats { columns: number; columnsWithoutFullCoverage: number; core: { p10: number; median: number }; weighted: { p10: number; median: number } }\ninterface RecordScheme {\n  background: string;\n  tokens: Record<'axis' | 'grid' | 'muted' | 'reference' | 'focus', string>;\n  lightness: { min: number; max: number; firstThreeSpread: number; maxChroma: number };\n  previewImage: Record<string, { pixels: string[]; min: number; pair: number[] }>;\n  series: { slot: number; hex: string; oklch: number[]; contrast: number; hueDiffToOtherScheme: number }[];\n  minDelta: Record<Vision, PairRecord>;\n  prefixes: Record<string, Record<Vision, PairRecord>>;\n  minDeltaBytes: Record<Vision, PairRecord>;\n  prefixesBytes: Record<string, Record<Vision, PairRecord>>;\n  tokenDistance: Record<string, { min: number; slot: number }>;\n  composites: Record<string, Record<Vision, { minNeighbour: number; pair: number[]; neighbours: number[]; ringClosures: { slots: number[]; d: number }[] }>>;\n  raster: Record<string, { model: string[]; modelFloor: string[]; neighboursModel: number[]; neighboursWorstWithinOneByte: number[] }>;\n  chromeRaster: Record<string, Record<string, { pixels: string[]; neighbours: number[]; min: number }>>;\n}\ninterface StrokeRecord { schemes: Record<'light' | 'dark', { background: string; rows: ({ color: string; swatch: number } & Record<string, StrokeStats | string | number>)[] }> }\nconst SCHEMES = [['light', LIGHT, DARK], ['dark', DARK, LIGHT]] as const;\nconst ALPHAS = [0.25, 0.8] as const;\nconst research = (file: string): string => path.resolve(import.meta.dirname, '../../../../research/palette', file);\nconst record = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(research('palette.json'), 'utf8')) as\n  { schemes: Record<'light' | 'dark', RecordScheme>; strokes: StrokeRecord; refused: string[]; oneByteEdits: Record<'light' | 'dark', { edits: number; stillMeetEveryCheck: number }> };\n/** The raw stroke measurement (strokes.mjs): per renderer, base64 of [count, channel bytes…] per pixel column. */\nconst strokesRaw = JSON.parse(gunzipSync(fs.readFileSync(research('strokes-raw.json.gz'))).toString('utf8')) as\n  { schemes: Record<'light' | 'dark', { background: string; rows: ({ color: string; channel: number } & Record<string, string | number>)[] }> };\nconst RENDERERS = ['canvas@1', 'svg@1', 'canvas@2', 'svg@2'] as const;\n\n/** Stroke statistics from raw channel bytes, independently of research/palette/color.mjs (same definition). */\nfunction strokeStats(ink: string, bg: string, ch: 0 | 1 | 2, encoded: string): StrokeStats {\n  const raw = Buffer.from(encoded, 'base64'), columns: number[][] = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < raw.length;) { const n = raw[i]!; columns.push([...raw.subarray(i + 1, i + 1 + n)]); i += 1 + n; }\n  const inkB = hexBytes(ink)[ch], bgB = hexBytes(bg)[ch], f = srgb(ink), b = srgb(bg);\n  const at = (k: number): number => {\n    const px = vec(i => Math.round(255 * (b[i] + k * (f[i] - b[i]))));\n    return contrastRatio({ r: px[0] / 255, g: px[1] / 255, b: px[2] / 255, a: 1 }, c(bg));\n  };\n  const used = columns.filter(vs => vs.length);\n  const cov = (vs: number[]): number[] => vs.map(v => Math.min(1, (v - bgB) / (inkB - bgB)));\n  const pct = (xs: number[]): { p10: number; median: number } => { xs.sort((x, y) => x - y); return { p10: xs[Math.floor(xs.length * 0.1)]!, median: xs[Math.floor(xs.length / 2)]! }; };\n  return {\n    columns: used.length,\n    columnsWithoutFullCoverage: used.filter(vs => !vs.includes(inkB)).length,\n    core: pct(used.map(vs => at(Math.max(...cov(vs))))),\n    weighted: pct(used.map(vs => { const k = cov(vs); return at(k.reduce((x, y) => x + y * y, 0) / k.reduce((x, y) => x + y, 0)); })),\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * Every required check of the decision for one scheme, as score.mjs applies them: contrast; pairwise and prefix\n * minima analytic and in bytes; hue match against the other scheme; token distances; fills ideal and on both\n * 8-bit raster models. Used to recount the one-byte-edit record.\n */\nfunction meetsEveryCheck(series: readonly string[], scheme: typeof LIGHT, otherSeries: readonly string[]): boolean {\n  const bg = c(scheme.background);\n  if (series.some(color => contrastRatio(c(color), bg) < 3)) return false;\n  for (const v of VISIONS) for (const labOf of [undefined, seenBytes]) {\n    if (closest(series, v, labOf).d < 0.10) return false;\n    for (const n of [3, 4, 5, 6]) if (closest(series.slice(0, n), v, labOf).d < (n === 4 ? 0.14 : 0.10)) return false;\n  }\n  if (series.some((color, k) => hueDifference(oklch(color)[2], oklch(otherSeries[k]!)[2]) > 30)) return false;\n  for (const token of ['focus', 'reference', 'muted'] as const) {\n    const t = seen(srgb(scheme[token]), 'normal');\n    if (series.some(color => delta(seen(srgb(color), 'normal'), t) < 0.10)) return false;\n  }\n  for (const alpha of ALPHAS) {\n    const labs = series.map(color => seen(over(color, alpha, scheme.background), 'normal'));\n    for (const quantise of [Math.round, Math.floor]) {\n      const px = series.map(color => labOfBytes(raster(color, alpha, scheme.background, quantise)));\n      for (let k = 0; k + 1 < 8; k++) if (delta(labs[k]!, labs[k + 1]!) < 0.06 || delta(px[k]!, px[k + 1]!) < 0.06) return false;\n    }\n  }\n  return true;\n}\n\n\ndescribe('scoring code used below', () => {\n  it('reproduces known OKLab values and leaves grays alone under every simulation', () => {\n    const white = seen([1, 1, 1], 'normal');\n    expect(white[0]).toBeCloseTo(1, 4);\n    expect(Math.hypot(white[1], white[2])).toBeLessThan(1e-4);\n    // Ottosson's reference value for sRGB red.\n    const red = seen([1, 0, 0], 'normal');\n    expect(red[0]).toBeCloseTo(0.6279554, 4);\n    expect(red[1]).toBeCloseTo(0.2248631, 4);\n    expect(red[2]).toBeCloseTo(0.1258463, 4);\n    for (const v of VISIONS) {\n      expect(delta(seen(srgb('#777777'), v), seen(srgb('#777777'), 'normal')), v).toBeLessThan(0.002);\n      // A gray survives simulation and byte rounding within one byte.\n      expect(delta(seenBytes('#777777', v), labOfBytes(hexBytes('#777777'))), v).toBeLessThan(0.004);\n    }\n    // Deuteranopia collapses red and green toward the same hue: the classic confusion pair gets much closer.\n    expect(delta(seen(srgb('#d62728'), 'deutan'), seen(srgb('#2ca02c'), 'deutan'))).toBeLessThan(0.5 * delta(seen(srgb('#d62728'), 'normal'), seen(srgb('#2ca02c'), 'normal')));\n  });\n\n  it('models an 8-bit fill within one byte of the pixels two rasterizers produced', () => {\n    // A CPU Skia raster of the review of 51e1be5 drew #5f2083 and #ba5f50 at 25% over white as #d7c8e0 and #eed7d4.\n    for (const [color, drawn] of [['#5f2083', '#d7c8e0'], ['#ba5f50', '#eed7d4']] as const) {\n      const m = raster(color, 0.25, '#ffffff'), d = hexBytes(drawn);\n      for (const i of [0, 1, 2] as const) expect(Math.abs(m[i] - d[i]), `${color} channel ${i}`).toBeLessThanOrEqual(1);\n    }\n    expect(worstWithinOneByte([10, 20, 30], [10, 20, 30])).toBe(0);\n    expect(worstWithinOneByte([10, 20, 30], [10, 20, 33])).toBeGreaterThan(0);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe('default palette (spec A5, decision 0003)', () => {\n  for (const [name, scheme, other] of SCHEMES) {\n    const series = scheme.series;\n    const r = record.schemes[name];\n\n    it(`${name}: eight distinct series colors`, () => {\n      expect(series).toHaveLength(8);\n      expect(new Set(series).size).toBe(8);\n      for (const color of series) expect(color).toMatch(/^#[0-9a-f]{6}$/);\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: 1. every series color reaches 3:1 on the default background`, () => {\n      const bg = c(scheme.background);\n      for (const color of series) expect(contrastRatio(c(color), bg), color).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(3);\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: 2. the eight are at least 0.10 apart in OKLab under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia, tritanopia`, () => {\n      for (const v of VISIONS) {\n        const { d, pair } = closest(series, v);\n        expect(d, `${v}: slots ${pair.join(' and ')}`).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0.10);\n      }\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: 2. the same after the simulated colors are rounded to bytes`, () => {\n      for (const v of VISIONS) {\n        const { d, pair } = closest(series, v, seenBytes);\n        expect(d, `${v}: slots ${pair.join(' and ')}`).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0.10);\n      }\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: 3. the first three, four, five and six are at least 0.10 apart, the first four at least 0.14 (analytic and in bytes)`, () => {\n      for (const n of [3, 4, 5, 6]) for (const v of VISIONS) for (const labOf of [undefined, seenBytes]) {\n        const { d, pair } = closest(series.slice(0, n), v, labOf);\n        expect(d, `first ${n}, ${v}${labOf ? ', bytes' : ''}: slots ${pair.join(' and ')}`).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(n === 4 ? 0.14 : 0.10);\n      }\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: 4. slot k is the same hue family in both schemes (OKLCH hue within 30°)`, () => {\n      series.forEach((color, k) => {\n        expect(hueDifference(oklch(color)[2], oklch(other.series[k]!)[2]), `slot ${k + 1}: ${color} / ${other.series[k]}`).toBeLessThanOrEqual(30);\n      });\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: 5. no series color is within 0.10 of the focus or reference token (nor muted, the donut's \"Other\" slice)`, () => {\n      for (const token of ['focus', 'reference', 'muted'] as const) {\n        const t = seen(srgb(scheme[token]), 'normal');\n        for (const color of series) expect(delta(seen(srgb(color), 'normal'), t), `${color} vs ${token} ${scheme[token]}`).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0.10);\n      }\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: 6. neighbours k and k+1 stay 0.06 apart as fills at 25% and 80% over the background`, () => {\n      for (const alpha of ALPHAS) {\n        const labs = series.map(color => seen(over(color, alpha, scheme.background), 'normal'));\n        for (let k = 0; k + 1 < labs.length; k++) {\n          expect(delta(labs[k]!, labs[k + 1]!), `${alpha * 100}%: slots ${k + 1} and ${k + 2}`).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0.06);\n        }\n      }\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: 6. the same on an 8-bit raster, with alpha quantised by rounding and by flooring`, () => {\n      for (const alpha of ALPHAS) for (const quantise of [Math.round, Math.floor]) {\n        const px = series.map(color => raster(color, alpha, scheme.background, quantise));\n        for (let k = 0; k + 1 < px.length; k++) {\n          expect(delta(labOfBytes(px[k]!), labOfBytes(px[k + 1]!)), `${alpha * 100}%, ${quantise.name}: slots ${k + 1} and ${k + 2}`).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0.06);\n        }\n      }\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: 6. the same on the pixels Chrome drew (research/palette/raster.json, canvas and SVG)`, () => {\n      expect(record.refused).toEqual([]);\n      expect(Object.keys(r.chromeRaster).sort()).toEqual(['canvas', 'svg']);\n      for (const [via, byAlpha] of Object.entries(r.chromeRaster)) {\n        expect(Object.keys(byAlpha).map(Number).sort()).toEqual([...ALPHAS]);\n        for (const alpha of ALPHAS) {\n          const drawn = byAlpha[String(alpha)]!.pixels;\n          expect(drawn).toHaveLength(8);\n          drawn.forEach((hex, k) => {\n            // The recorded pixel is a fill of this slot's color: within one byte of the model.\n            const m = raster(series[k]!, alpha, scheme.background), d = hexBytes(hex);\n            for (const i of [0, 1, 2] as const) expect(Math.abs(m[i] - d[i]), `${via} ${alpha} slot ${k + 1}`).toBeLessThanOrEqual(1);\n          });\n          for (let k = 0; k + 1 < drawn.length; k++) {\n            expect(delta(labOfBytes(hexBytes(drawn[k]!)), labOfBytes(hexBytes(drawn[k + 1]!))), `${via} ${alpha * 100}%: slots ${k + 1} and ${k + 2}`).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0.06);\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: the research record holds these colors, its tokens, and the measured fields listed in research/palette/README.md at their written precision`, () => {\n      expect(r.background).toBe(scheme.background);\n      expect(r.tokens).toEqual({ axis: scheme.axis, grid: scheme.grid, muted: scheme.muted, reference: scheme.reference, focus: scheme.focus });\n      const Ls = series.map(color => oklch(color)[0]), Cs = series.map(color => oklch(color)[1]);\n      expect(atPrecision(Math.min(...Ls), r.lightness.min, 3) && atPrecision(Math.max(...Ls), r.lightness.max, 3), 'lightness range').toBe(true);\n      expect(atPrecision(Math.max(...Ls.slice(0, 3)) - Math.min(...Ls.slice(0, 3)), r.lightness.firstThreeSpread, 3), 'first-three spread').toBe(true);\n      expect(atPrecision(Math.max(...Cs), r.lightness.maxChroma, 3), 'largest chroma').toBe(true);\n      expect(r.series.map(s => s.hex)).toEqual([...series]);\n      series.forEach((color, k) => {\n        const s = r.series[k]!, [L, C, h] = oklch(color);\n        expect(s.slot).toBe(k + 1);\n        expect(atPrecision(L, s.oklch[0]!, 3) && atPrecision(C, s.oklch[1]!, 3) && atPrecision(h, s.oklch[2]!, 1), `${color} OKLCH ${s.oklch.join(' ')}`).toBe(true);\n        expect(atPrecision(contrastRatio(c(color), c(scheme.background)), s.contrast, 2), `${color} contrast ${s.contrast}`).toBe(true);\n        expect(atPrecision(hueDifference(h, oklch(other.series[k]!)[2]), s.hueDiffToOtherScheme, 1), `slot ${k + 1} hue shift`).toBe(true);\n      });\n      const pairs = (measured: { d: number; pair: number[] }, rec: PairRecord, label: string): void => {\n        expect(atPrecision(measured.d, rec.min, 3), `${label}: ${measured.d} vs ${rec.min}`).toBe(true);\n        expect(rec.pair, label).toEqual(measured.pair);\n      };\n      for (const v of VISIONS) {\n        pairs(closest(series, v), r.minDelta[v], `all 8, ${v}`);\n        pairs(closest(series, v, seenBytes), r.minDeltaBytes[v], `all 8, ${v}, bytes`);\n        for (const n of [3, 4, 5, 6]) {\n          pairs(closest(series.slice(0, n), v), r.prefixes[n]![v], `first ${n}, ${v}`);\n          pairs(closest(series.slice(0, n), v, seenBytes), r.prefixesBytes[n]![v], `first ${n}, ${v}, bytes`);\n        }\n      }\n      for (const token of ['focus', 'reference', 'muted'] as const) {\n        const t = seen(srgb(scheme[token]), 'normal'), ds = series.map(color => delta(seen(srgb(color), 'normal'), t));\n        const min = Math.min(...ds);\n        expect(atPrecision(min, r.tokenDistance[token]!.min, 3), token).toBe(true);\n        expect(r.tokenDistance[token]!.slot, token).toBe(ds.indexOf(min) + 1);\n      }\n      for (const alpha of ALPHAS) {\n        const key = String(alpha);\n        for (const v of VISIONS) {\n          // Fills under every vision, neighbours and the closing pairs of a donut ring (recorded, not required).\n          const labs = series.map(color => oklab(simulated(over(color, alpha, scheme.background), v)));\n          const nb = labs.slice(1).map((l, k) => delta(labs[k]!, l));\n          const rec = r.composites[key]![v];\n          nb.forEach((d, k) => expect(atPrecision(d, rec.neighbours[k]!, 3), `${alpha} ${v} neighbours ${k + 1}`).toBe(true));\n          expect(atPrecision(Math.min(...nb), rec.minNeighbour, 3), `${alpha} ${v}`).toBe(true);\n          expect(rec.pair).toEqual([nb.indexOf(Math.min(...nb)) + 1, nb.indexOf(Math.min(...nb)) + 2]);\n          const ring = [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8].map(n => ({ slots: [n, 1], d: delta(labs[n - 1]!, labs[0]!) }));\n          expect(rec.ringClosures.map(x => x.slots)).toEqual(ring.map(x => x.slots));\n          ring.forEach((x, k) => expect(atPrecision(x.d, rec.ringClosures[k]!.d, 3), `${alpha} ${v} ring ${x.slots.join('-')}`).toBe(true));\n        }\n        const px = series.map(color => raster(color, alpha, scheme.background));\n        const hex = (p: Vec3): string => '#' + p.map(v => v.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\n        expect(r.raster[key]!.model).toEqual(px.map(hex));\n        expect(r.raster[key]!.modelFloor).toEqual(series.map(color => hex(raster(color, alpha, scheme.background, Math.floor))));\n        px.slice(1).forEach((p, k) => {\n          expect(atPrecision(delta(labOfBytes(px[k]!), labOfBytes(p)), r.raster[key]!.neighboursModel[k]!, 3), `${alpha} model ${k + 1}`).toBe(true);\n          expect(atPrecision(worstWithinOneByte(px[k]!, p), r.raster[key]!.neighboursWorstWithinOneByte[k]!, 3), `${alpha} worst ${k + 1}`).toBe(true);\n        });\n        for (const via of ['canvas', 'svg']) {\n          const x = r.chromeRaster[via]![key]!;\n          const d = x.pixels.slice(1).map((p, k) => delta(labOfBytes(hexBytes(x.pixels[k]!)), labOfBytes(hexBytes(p))));\n          d.forEach((v, k) => expect(atPrecision(v, x.neighbours[k]!, 3), `${via} ${alpha} ${k + 1}`).toBe(true));\n          expect(atPrecision(Math.min(...d), x.min, 3)).toBe(true);\n        }\n      }\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: the 2 px stroke record is these colors, and every statistic in it follows from the raw pixel record`, () => {\n      const s = record.strokes.schemes[name], raw = strokesRaw.schemes[name];\n      expect(s.background).toBe(scheme.background);\n      expect(raw.background).toBe(scheme.background);\n      expect(s.rows.map(row => row.color)).toEqual([...series]);\n      expect(raw.rows.map(row => row.color)).toEqual([...series]);\n      s.rows.forEach((row, k) => {\n        const rawRow = raw.rows[k]!, ch = rawRow.channel as 0 | 1 | 2;\n        expect(atPrecision(contrastRatio(c(row.color), c(scheme.background)), row.swatch, 2), `${row.color} swatch`).toBe(true);\n        // The deciding channel is the one where ink and background differ most.\n        const ink = hexBytes(row.color), back = hexBytes(scheme.background);\n        expect(Math.abs(ink[ch] - back[ch])).toBe(Math.max(...[0, 1, 2].map(i => Math.abs(ink[i as 0] - back[i as 0]))));\n        for (const key of RENDERERS) {\n          const st = row[key] as StrokeStats, again = strokeStats(row.color, scheme.background, ch, rawRow[key] as string);\n          expect(st.columns, `${row.color} ${key}`).toBe(again.columns);\n          expect(st.columnsWithoutFullCoverage, `${row.color} ${key}`).toBe(again.columnsWithoutFullCoverage);\n          for (const stat of ['core', 'weighted'] as const) for (const q of ['p10', 'median'] as const) {\n            expect(atPrecision(again[stat][q], st[stat][q], 2), `${row.color} ${key} ${stat} ${q}: ${again[stat][q]} vs ${st[stat][q]}`).toBe(true);\n          }\n        }\n      });\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: the preview PNG itself (donut slices read back from each row) matches the palette and keeps 0.10 apart`, () => {\n      const img = r.previewImage, rows = { normal: 'normal', protanopia: 'protan', deuteranopia: 'deutan', tritanopia: 'tritan' } as const;\n      expect(img.normal!.pixels).toEqual([...series]);\n      for (const [row, v] of Object.entries(rows)) {\n        const px = img[row]!.pixels;\n        px.forEach((hex, k) => {\n          // The image is the byte-rounding model within one byte per channel (Chrome's filter rounds differently).\n          const model = vec(i => Math.round(255 * enc(simulated(srgb(series[k]!), v)[i]))), d = hexBytes(hex);\n          for (const i of [0, 1, 2] as const) expect(Math.abs(model[i] - d[i]), `${row} slot ${k + 1}`).toBeLessThanOrEqual(1);\n        });\n        const labs = px.map(hex => labOfBytes(hexBytes(hex)));\n        let min = Infinity, pair = [0, 0];\n        for (let i = 0; i < 8; i++) for (let j = i + 1; j < 8; j++) { const d = delta(labs[i]!, labs[j]!); if (d < min) { min = d; pair = [i + 1, j + 1]; } }\n        expect(min, `${row}: slots ${pair.join(' and ')}`).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0.10);\n        expect(atPrecision(min, img[row]!.min, 3)).toBe(true);\n        expect(img[row]!.pair).toEqual(pair);\n      }\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: the one-byte-edit count in the record is what every check gives for every one-byte edit`, () => {\n      let edits = 0, pass = 0;\n      series.forEach((hex, k) => {\n        for (const ch of [0, 1, 2] as const) for (const d of [-1, 1]) {\n          const b = [...hexBytes(hex)]; b[ch] = b[ch]! + d;\n          if (b[ch]! < 0 || b[ch]! > 255) continue;\n          const edited = series.slice(); edited[k] = '#' + b.map(v => v.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\n          edits++;\n          if (meetsEveryCheck(edited, scheme, other.series)) pass++;\n        }\n      });\n      expect(meetsEveryCheck(series, scheme, other.series)).toBe(true);\n      expect(record.oneByteEdits[name]).toEqual({ edits, stillMeetEveryCheck: pass });\n    });\n\n    it(`${name}: token colors meet spec L2 (axis and focus 3:1; muted and reference 4.5:1, they label text)`, () => {\n      const bg = c(scheme.background);\n      expect(contrastRatio(c(scheme.axis), bg)).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(3);\n      expect(contrastRatio(c(scheme.focus), bg)).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(3);\n      expect(contrastRatio(c(scheme.muted), bg)).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(4.5);\n      expect(contrastRatio(c(scheme.reference), bg)).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(4.5);\n    });\n  }\n});\n\ndescribe('default stylesheet', () => {\n  it('defines the light and dark token sets, forced colors and the dash switch', () => {\n    for (let i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {\n      // Public names today, private defaults (--axylo-default-series-N) once the review's stylesheet change lands.\n      expect(AXYLO_CSS).toMatch(new RegExp(`--axylo-(default-)?series-${i}: ${LIGHT.series[i - 1]};`));\n      expect(AXYLO_CSS).toMatch(new RegExp(`--axylo-(default-)?series-${i}: ${DARK.series[i - 1]};`));\n      expect(AXYLO_CSS).toContain(`--axylo-series-${i}: CanvasText !important;`);\n    }\n    expect(AXYLO_CSS).toContain('.axylo[data-axylo-scheme=\"dark\"]');\n    expect(AXYLO_CSS).toContain('@media (forced-colors: active)');\n    expect(AXYLO_CSS).toMatch(/@media \\(prefers-contrast: more\\), \\(forced-colors: active\\) \\{\\s*(\\.axylo )?\\.axylo-svg \\.axylo-dashed \\{ stroke-dasharray: var\\(--axylo-dash-contrast/);\n    expect(AXYLO_CSS).toContain('--axylo-stylesheet: loaded');\n  });\n\n  it('never sets what scheme detection reads (color-scheme, color, --axylo-background) outside forced colors', () => {\n    const outsideForced = AXYLO_CSS.replace(/@media \\(forced-colors: active\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n {2}\\}\\n/, '');\n    expect(outsideForced).not.toMatch(/color-scheme\\s*:/);\n    expect(outsideForced).not.toMatch(/--axylo-background\\s*:/);\n    expect(outsideForced).not.toMatch(/\\.axylo\\s*\\{[^}]*[^-]color\\s*:/);\n  });\n\n  it('keeps the marker that separates the figure module\\'s structure rules from the token and mark part', () => {\n    const marker = AXYLO_CSS.indexOf('/* structure: added by the figure module');\n    expect(marker).toBeGreaterThan(AXYLO_CSS.indexOf('.axylo-text'));\n  });\n\n  it('balances its braces', () => {\n    expect(AXYLO_CSS.split('{').length).toBe(AXYLO_CSS.split('}').length);\n  });\n});\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
