{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat research/palette/configs/fit-final.json | head -24 && sed -n '143,158p' packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts && nl -ba docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md | rg 'No single|0.028|currently|every sampled|by up to 0.09|decision record|red and orange|PNG|neighbour|every required check|0.23|0.207|the same family|spread across|1 px|not at the edge'\"","aggregated_output":"{\n \"note\": \"Shipped palette: candidate B, adopted by the owner on 2026-11-18 (decision 0003). Same boxes as configs/candidate-b.json, which is kept as the record of the candidate. Refit from the second review's pair by eye: slot 6 moved from a second blue to cyan and teal hues (configs/b-cyan.json, b-cyan-45.json: light floor L 0.45), neutral eighth at chroma 0.015 or less, dark red and orange held to chroma 0.15 or less. Light: the exact local search around the best b-cyan-45 annealing state; dark: the exact search against that light set with a lighter blue and a calmer cyan asked for. Both refitted here inside small boxes with the solver margins (fills 0.063, pairwise 0.102 analytic and in bytes; contrast 3.02), so both searches complete and the run reproduces the shipped arrays in candidates/fit-final.json. Same tokens as the shipped palette.\",\n \"hueClip\": 15,\n \"solverLimits\": {\n  \"composite\": 0.063,\n  \"minDelta\": 0.102\n },\n \"tokens\": {\n  \"dark\": {\n   \"focus\": \"#e3e8f0\"\n  }\n },\n \"slots\": [\n  {\n   \"name\": \"blue\",\n   \"neutral\": false,\n   \"light\": {\n    \"target\": [\n     0.6593,\n     0.16,\n     248.13\n    ],\n    \"L\": [\n     0.6393,\n\n/**\n * Every required check of the decision for one scheme, as score.mjs applies them: contrast; pairwise and prefix\n * minima analytic and in bytes; hue match against the other scheme; token distances; fills ideal and on both\n * 8-bit raster models. Used to recount the one-byte-edit record.\n */\nfunction meetsEveryCheck(series: readonly string[], scheme: typeof LIGHT, otherSeries: readonly string[]): boolean {\n  const bg = c(scheme.background);\n  if (series.some(color => contrastRatio(c(color), bg) < 3)) return false;\n  for (const v of VISIONS) for (const labOf of [undefined, seenBytes]) {\n    if (closest(series, v, labOf).d < 0.10) return false;\n    for (const n of [3, 4, 5, 6]) if (closest(series.slice(0, n), v, labOf).d < (n === 4 ? 0.14 : 0.10)) return false;\n  }\n  if (series.some((color, k) => hueDifference(oklch(color)[2], oklch(otherSeries[k]!)[2]) > 30)) return false;\n  for (const token of ['focus', 'reference', 'muted'] as const) {\n    const t = seen(srgb(scheme[token]), 'normal');\n    45\t| Light, spread across the first three | 0.26 | 0.25 | 0.20 |\n    52\t| 25% fills on an 8-bit raster, worst neighbours | 0.0590 (review's raster) | 0.063 (Chrome) | 0.064 light, 0.063 dark (Chrome) |\n    73\t- the swatches, with hex, contrast and nearest neighbour;\n    88\t  - **The rounding model is not the preview image.** Chrome's SVG filter can differ from the model by a byte. For the shipped palette, every sampled donut pixel of the preview equals the model. For the superseded palette one did not: in light tritanopia, slot 7 was `#b30f41` in the PNG and `#b20f41` in the model, which took the light tritan 5–7 distance from 0.102 in the model to 0.1005 in the image.\n    91\t  - the preview PNG itself: `preview.mjs` reads back the middle of every donut slice in every row of the image it just wrote (`preview-pixels.json`).\n   109\t| 2, image | the same on the donut slices read back from the preview PNG | ≥ 0.10 | 0.113 / 0.103 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.115 / 0.105 / 0.102 / 0.103 |\n   114\t| 6 | neighbour fills at 25%: ideal / byte models / Chrome canvas / Chrome SVG | ≥ 0.06 | 0.065 / 0.064 / 0.064 / 0.064 | 0.064 / 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 |\n   115\t| 6 | neighbour fills at 80%: ideal / byte models / Chrome | ≥ 0.06 | 0.223 / 0.224 / 0.224 | 0.175 / 0.174 / 0.175 |\n   128\t| first 3 | deutan | 0.235 / 0.235 (2–3) | 0.208 / 0.208 (2–3) |\n   154\t- **Fills, both schemes:** the 25% neighbours have about 0.003–0.005 of room; the 80% fills have a lot.\n   156\tHow fragile is that to a hand edit? I tried every one-byte change of one channel of one color. 44 of 48 light edits and 48 of 48 dark edits still meet every required check (`palette.json`, `oneByteEdits`). A changed hex still fails the record test until `score.mjs` is rerun.\n   158\tThe solver kept margins above the tested limits: pairwise 0.102, fills 0.063, contrast 3.02, and a hue shift of 15° (`configs/fit-final.json`, `solverLimits`). B is not at the edge of its boxes: with fills at 0.065 and pairwise at 0.103, the same boxes still hold solutions that differ by a few bytes in several colors. I kept B as chosen and did not search for the largest margins.\n   163\t- **Dark.** `#e3e8f0` is under 3:1 against five dark series colors: blue 2.27, orange 2.54, green 1.17, cyan 1.58 and pink 1.96. Purple (4.70), red (4.04) and slate (3.94) pass. No single ink reaches 3:1 against both `#16181d` and the light green; even white reaches only 1.44:1 against it.\n   187\t- **Its dark red shouts.** `#fe573f` (chroma 0.207 at L 0.68) is the loudest mark in the dark scheme.\n   192\t- held the dark red and orange at chroma 0.15 or less.\n   205\t| Light largest chroma | 0.205 (purple) | 0.207 (purple, red) | 0.220 (purple) |\n   208\t| Dark largest chroma | 0.177 (purple) | 0.207 (red) | 0.171 (purple) |\n   215\t  - The light purple is at chroma 0.220, above both other sets. In B's neighbourhood, a cap of 0.207 is proved infeasible: the exact search in `configs/b-purple-207.json` completes with no solution.\n   216\t  - Calming the red pushes purple up instead. Red at 0.18 or 0.17 is feasible only with purple at 0.23 (`b-red-18.json`, `b-red-17.json`), and red at 0.16 is proved infeasible (`b-red-16.json`).\n   229\t**The candidate's own records.** These are `palette-b.json`, `raster-b.json`, `strokes-b.json`, `strokes-b-raw.json.gz`, `preview-b-*.png` and `preview-b-pixels.json`, all written before adoption. I regenerated the shipped records from `palette.ts` in Chrome 157. The result is identical to the candidate's records byte for byte: the raster, stroke and raw stroke records, the preview pixels and the light PNG. `palette.json` equals `palette-b.json` except for the latter's `candidate` field.\n   264\t- **Renderers.** Canvas and SVG differ by up to 0.09 (dark green, DPR 1 p10: 8.50 canvas, 8.41 SVG), and most often by 0.00–0.05.\n   286\t- **Fills:** the 25% neighbours were 0.063 in Chrome, and 0.056 at the worst case within one byte.\n   290\t**Its record.** That commit holds its Chrome records and preview PNGs. `configs/fit-superseded.json` reproduces its arrays; I ran it again here and both searches completed. It came out of a four-step refit, and an explorer fix shipped alongside:\n   293\t2. **Annealing.** `anneal.mjs` searches the same grids by minimising the total shortfall. I checked it first on a known-feasible neighbourhood, where it recovered the first review's palette.\n   314\tIt lay outside the boxes my what-if runs had searched. That pair pays in chroma: its magenta has chroma 0.30, its red 0.21 and its purple 0.23, and one hue shift sits at 29.97°. The second review then found a pair with light L 0.460–0.673 and chroma at most 0.207. So the uneven lightness of the superseded set was not the price of restrained chroma either. B starts from that pair.\n   328\t- **A palette with every light color at L 0.48 or above that also keeps chroma at 0.22 or less**: none found. My conclusion at the time was that the lightness-for-chroma trade was real. That was wrong: the second review found an L 0.46 floor at chroma 0.207 by searching a wider range of hues. My searches were limited in time, boxes and hue ranges.\n   333\t- **Byte-level checks** (tested): byte-rounded simulation; fills on both 8-bit raster models; Chrome's drawn pixels within one byte of the model and 0.06 apart; the preview PNG's donut pixels 0.10 apart.\n   336\t  - Neighbours under simulation go down to 0.028 (light, deuteranopia, 6–7).\n   396\t  - the preview PNG's pixels;\n   402\t  - Previews: `preview.html` and the two PNGs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
