{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"node -e 'console.log(Object.keys(require(\\\"./node_modules/playwright-core/lib/utilsBundle.js\\\")))' && sed -n '63,109p' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/misc-opus.review.md && nl -ba docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md | sed -n '317,354p'\"","aggregated_output":"[\n  'CallToolRequestSchema',\n  'Client',\n  'HttpsProxyAgent',\n  'ListRootsRequestSchema',\n  'ListToolsRequestSchema',\n  'PNG',\n  'PingRequestSchema',\n  'ProgramOption',\n  'ProgressNotificationSchema',\n  'SSEClientTransport',\n  'SSEServerTransport',\n  'Server',\n  'SocksProxyAgent',\n  'StdioClientTransport',\n  'StdioServerTransport',\n  'StreamableHTTPClientTransport',\n  'StreamableHTTPServerTransport',\n  'chokidar',\n  'colors',\n  'debug',\n  'diff',\n  'dotenv',\n  'enquirer',\n  'getEastAsianWidth',\n  'getProxyForUrl',\n  'getStream',\n  'gracefulFs',\n  'httpProxyAgent',\n  'ini',\n  'jpegjs',\n  'json5',\n  'mime',\n  'minimatch',\n  'onExit',\n  'open',\n  'program',\n  'progress',\n  'retry',\n  'socksProxyAgent',\n  'sourceMapSupport',\n  'ws',\n  'wsReceiver',\n  'wsSender',\n  'wsServer',\n  'yaml',\n  'yauzl',\n  'yazl',\n  'z',\n  'zodToJsonSchema'\n]\n## B. Palette refit · `52bbdf0`\n\n### Findings\n\n1. **B · The new dark focus token fails the adjacent-colour target. Ran WCAG contrast arithmetic and traced the focus geometry.** `#e3e8f0` gives **14.433:1** on `#16181d`. Against dark slots 1–8 it gives **1.934, 2.446, 1.195, 4.424, 3.739, 1.316 and 1.682 and 3.230:1**, respectively. Blue, orange, green, cyan and pink fail. The square datum outline can cross neighbouring marks and uses a single focus ink. Its OKLab separation cannot establish this contrast. In fact, the background and purple force a light single ink, and even white cannot reach 3:1 against the green. **Fix:** use a visible two-tone outline or a guaranteed background surround, for both the plot and datum indicators where they can cross marks. Check the actual adjacent colours. WCAG **1.4.11** supplies the adjacent-colour test. **2.4.11** is Focus Not Obscured and requires a layout check. **2.4.13** separately compares the same pixels in focused and unfocused states. [Token](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts:45) · [Datum outline](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:252) · [WCAG 1.4.11](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html) · [WCAG 2.4.11](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-not-obscured-minimum.html) · [WCAG 2.4.13](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-appearance.html)\n\n2. **m · The simulated preview has a smaller margin than the byte model. Ran pixel extraction on the committed PNGs.** Light tritan slot 7 is `#b30f41` in the preview and `#b20f41` in the independently rounded model. The light tritan 5–7 distance is **0.100475** in the image versus **0.102175** in the model. The PNG still clears 0.10, and every other sampled channel matched. **Fix:** distinguish the image from the rounding model in the decision and retain a PNG-interior check. [Decision’s byte claim](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:55) · [Preview filters](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/preview.html:18)\n\n3. **m · The record checks cover fewer fields than they claim. Ran mutations in memory.** A wrong closest pair, a **0.01** contrast change, zeroed OKLCH coordinates and a stroke median of 99 each failed. All **32** palette tests still passed with a plausible but false stroke p10/median of **1.01/1.02**, and with false stored token, simulated-fill, lightness and edit-count fields. The scorer checks browser-record colours and backgrounds before importing them, but it rounds its Chrome distance before its pass/fail comparison. **Fix:** check the published fields against their independent calculation or the pinned raw measurement record, and apply the limit before rounding. Narrow the test’s “every measured field” claim to the fields it verifies. [Record test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts:234) · [Stroke assertions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts:287) · [Scorer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/score.mjs:68)\n\n4. **m · Two corrected passages still overstate their evidence. Ran the record comparison and reasoned from the stroke sampler.** The dark green DPR 1 weighted p10 is **8.32 Canvas / 8.23 SVG**, a **0.09** difference against the stated maximum of 0.05. Dark cyan differs by 0.07. Rounded core p10 equality does not recover the full-coverage count. The dark first-three normal closest pair is **2–3** at **0.251291**. The JSON is correct and the decision says **1–3**. The source palette comment also retains “left no room” for the former focus blue after the decision qualified that result as a timed search. **Fix:** label the stroke rows by renderer, retain raw coverage if claiming a count, correct the pair, and use the bounded search claim in the source comment. [Prefix table](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:86) · [Stroke conclusion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:122) · [Palette comment](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts:11)\n\n### Independent measurements\n\nI used linear sRGB → D65 XYZ → LMS → OKLab, WCAG luminance and full-severity Machado matrices. I rescored the recorded fill pixels directly. The independently rounded JSON values and pair identities agreed for the series, prefixes, token distances and fill rows I recomputed.\n\n| Slot | Light OKLCH | Light contrast | Dark OKLCH | Dark contrast | Hue shift |\n|---|---|---:|---|---:|---:|\n| 1 | .65984 / .14968 / 254.09° | 3.12186 | .73054 / .13525 / 251.85° | 7.46150 | 2.24° |\n| 2 | .67998 / .17508 / 51.91° | 3.05603 | .68092 / .15018 / 53.98° | 5.90148 | 2.07° |\n| 3 | .43000 / .14017 / 141.05° | 7.66859 | .86186 / .12085 / 133.26° | 12.07884 | 7.79° |\n| 4 | .43462 / .20512 / 310.08° | 8.93329 | .54393 / .17690 / 311.58° | 3.26215 | 1.50° |\n| 5 | .58678 / .13026 / 18.31° | 4.44794 | .57968 / .15057 / 31.95° | 3.86024 | 13.64° |\n| 6 | .54943 / .11522 / 234.15° | 4.75798 | .83482 / .11017 / 225.24° | 10.96692 | 8.91° |\n| 7 | .48859 / .17504 / 354.96° | 6.96149 | .78591 / .11937 / 0.71° | 8.58329 | 5.75° |\n| 8 | .66837 / .02645 / 283.13° | 3.02637 | .59924 / .03095 / 274.34° | 4.46895 | 8.79° |\n\nPrefix minima below are **analytic / byte-rounded**, in normal, protan, deutan and tritan order.\n\n| Prefix | Light | Dark |\n|---|---|---|\n| First 3 | .31936/.31936 · .15557/.15541 · .26996/.27056 · .24313/.24141 | .25129/.25129 · .24686/.24606 · .17800/.17750 · .15849/.15906 |\n| First 4 | .28436/.28436 · .15557/.15541 · .21746/.21728 · .15730/.15747 | .24553/.24553 · .24686/.24606 · .17800/.17750 · .15849/.15906 |\n| First 5 | .13533/.13533 · .10289/.10281 · .13599/.13716 · .10227/.10300 | .11640/.11640 · .12566/.12503 · .10852/.10869 · .10337/.10214 |\n| First 6 | .12427/.12427 · .10289/.10281 · .11728/.11720 · .10227/.10290 | .11640/.11640 · .12392/.12411 · .10852/.10869 · .10337/.10214 |\n| All 8 | .12403/.12403 · .10289/.10281 · .10342/.10345 · .10227/.10217 | .11640/.11640 · .10370/.10304 · .10301/.10217 · .10233/.10214 |\n\n| Other check | Light | Dark |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Nearest focus / reference / muted | .100772 / .152348 / .110171 | .138042 / .104214 / .113348 |\n| 25% fill, ideal / round-alpha / floor-alpha / Chrome Canvas / Chrome SVG | .063696 / .063379 / .063351 / .063379 / .063379 | .063836 / .063524 / .064321 / .062652 / .062652 |\n| 80% fill, same order | .201393 / .201561 / .201561 / .201561 / .201561 | .178653 / .179037 / .179037 / .179152 / .179152 |\n| 25% fill, worst within one byte per channel | .056008 | .056477 |\n\nI also imported the installed `d3-scale-chromatic` **3.1.1** exports directly. The white-background counts below 3:1 reproduced as **5/10, 6/10, 4/10, 2/8 and 5/9** for Tableau 10, Observable 10, Category 10, Dark2 and Set1. All five first-eight distance rows reproduced at published precision, including Tableau’s red–green deutan distance of **0.006640**. [Reference record](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/references.json)\n\n### More even light scheme\n   317\t\n   318\tEach line below means \"not found\" in the searches for the superseded refit; B's own searches are listed in its section above. Only searches that complete prove anything, and those are marked.\n   319\t\n   320\t- **A lighter, calmer slot-3 green** (L 0.46 or above, chroma 0.11 or below), with restrained chroma elsewhere (`configs/green-a.json`; `green-b.json`, which also allowed a saturated red): nothing at the time.\n   321\t  - The shortfalls were green against red under protanopia, and magenta against cyan and slate.\n   322\t  - B later found one (`#0b6c49`, L 0.47, chroma 0.10), with wider hue boxes and a purple at chroma 0.22.\n   323\t- **Walking from the first review's palette toward lower chroma** while staying feasible (`configs/walk-b.json`): the green and red calmed down, but the magenta stayed at chroma 0.28 or more (`#fd26e0`).\n   324\t- **The first review's tier structure with chroma held at 0.22 or less** (`configs/cx-a.json`): nothing; magenta was always the shortfall.\n   325\t- **Red third**, as in Tableau 10 (`configs/red3-a.json`): nothing; red was short against green under protanopia.\n   326\t- **Magenta fourth instead of purple**, to relieve the dark blue (`configs/mag4-a.json`): feasible on white. Its dark set was proved infeasible with the old focus token (exact search, `configs/dark-n2-m4.json`), and annealing found none with the new one (`configs/dark-b2-m4.json`).\n   327\t- **Fills at the worst case within one byte per channel, in both schemes**: never met together with the other goals. B meets it in light (0.060) but not in dark (0.057).\n   328\t- **A palette with every light color at L 0.48 or above that also keeps chroma at 0.22 or less**: none found. My conclusion at the time was that the lightness-for-chroma trade was real. That was wrong: the second review found an L 0.46 floor at chroma 0.207 by searching a wider range of hues. My searches were limited in time, boxes and hue ranges.\n   329\t\n   330\t## Additions beyond the six constraints\n   331\t\n   332\t- **Muted token** in constraint 5 (tested). The donut's \"Other\" slice.\n   333\t- **Byte-level checks** (tested): byte-rounded simulation; fills on both 8-bit raster models; Chrome's drawn pixels within one byte of the model and 0.06 apart; the preview PNG's donut pixels 0.10 apart.\n   334\t- **Ring closure and deficiency-simulated fills** (recorded only).\n   335\t  - At 25% the donut's closing pairs go down to 0.029 (light 6–1) and 0.037 (dark 6–1).\n   336\t  - Neighbours under simulation go down to 0.028 (light, deuteranopia, 6–7).\n   337\t  - Slices are opaque in Axylo, so constraint 2 covers them.\n   338\t- **Solver margins** as listed above. The tests use the decision's limits.\n   339\t\n   340\t## For the owner\n   341\t\n   342\t- **The light red and purple are the loudest light colors** (chroma 0.20 and 0.22). Calming either costs the other (\"What B costs\").\n   343\t- **The dark focus ring is near-white `#e3e8f0`** (light stays blue).\n   344\t  - This is a visible UI change, made to free the dark blue.\n   345\t  - With the two-tone ring it meets its contrast need (14.4:1 on the background).\n   346\t  - Reverting to `#79a8ff` is possible under two tones only if constraint 5 is dropped: it is 0.050 from the dark blue (see \"Focus token under a two-tone ring\").\n   347\t- **The dark set mixes three very light colors with two dimmer ones.** The light ones are the green (L 0.87), cyan (0.78) and pink (0.74). The dimmer ones are a deep purple (0.53) and a brick red (0.56), the dimmest at 3.07 and 3.57:1.\n   348\t- **Slot hues move within each family between schemes.** The largest shift is 14.1° (purple), under constraint 4's 30°.\n   349\t\n   350\t## Reference palettes (measured)\n   351\t\n   352\tThese are read from the installed d3-scale-chromatic 3.1.1 (`research/harness/node_modules`) by `research/palette/references.mjs` and scored with the same code (`research/palette/references.json`). Minima are over the first eight colors.\n   353\t\n   354\t| Scheme | Below 3:1 on white | Min ΔE normal / protan / deutan / tritan (closest slots) |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"node --input-type=module <<'NODE'\nimport fs from 'node:fs';\nimport { LIGHT as light,DARK as dark } from './packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts';\nconst json = f => JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('research/palette/'+f+'.json','utf8'));\nconst schemes={light,dark}, record=json('palette');\nconst rgb=s=>s.match(/[a-f\\\\d]{2}/ig).map(x=>parseInt(x,16));\nconst hex=b=>'#'+b.map(x=>x.toString(16).padStart(2,'0')).join('');\nconst clamp=x=>Math.max(0,Math.min(1,x)), vlin=x=>x<=0.04045?x/12.92:((x+0.055)/1.055)**2.4, sr=x=>x<=0.0031308?12.92*x:1.055*x**(1/2.4)-0.055;\nconst dot=(x,y)=>x.reduce((t,v,i)=>t+v*y[i],0), product=(m,v)=>m.map(row=>dot(row,v));\nconst xmat=[[0.4123907992659595,0.357584339383878,0.1804807884018343],[0.2126390058715104,0.715168678767756,0.0721923153607337],[0.0193308187155918,0.119194779794626,0.9505321522496607]];\nconst cone=[[0.819022437996703,0.3619062600528904,-0.1288737815209879],[0.0329836539323885,0.9292868615863434,0.0361446663506424],[0.0481771893596242,0.2642395317527308,0.6335478284694309]];\nconst opp=[[0.2104542553,0.7936177850,-0.0040720468],[1.9779984951,-2.4285922050,0.4505937099],[0.0259040371,0.7827717662,-0.8086757660]];\nconst mach={N:[[1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1]],P:[[.152286,1.052583,-.204868],[.114503,.786281,.099216],[-.003882,-.048116,1.051998]],D:[[.367322,.860646,-.227968],[.280085,.672501,.047413],[-.011820,.042940,.968881]],T:[[1.255528,-.076749,-.178779],[-.078411,.930809,.147602],[.004733,.691367,.303900]]};\nconst vis=Object.keys(mach), vnames={N:'normal',P:'protan',D:'deutan',T:'tritan'}, pnames={N:'normal',P:'protanopia',D:'deuteranopia',T:'tritanopia'};\nconst sim=(b,v='N')=>product(mach[v],b.map(c=>vlin(c/255))).map(clamp), quant=l=>l.map(x=>Math.round(255*sr(x)));\nconst lab=(b,v='N',q=false)=>{let l=sim(b,v); if(q) l=quant(l).map(x=>vlin(x/255));return product(opp,product(cone,product(xmat,l)).map(Math.cbrt))};\nconst polar = b => {const [l,a,c]=lab(b);return [l,Math.hypot(a,c),(Math.atan2(c,a)*180/Math.PI+360)%360]};\nconst lum=b=>dot(b.map(c=>vlin(c/255)),[.2126,.7152,.0722]);\nconst cr=(a,b)=>(Math.max(lum(a),lum(b))+.05)/(Math.min(lum(a),lum(b))+.05);\nconst diff=(a,b)=>Math.hypot(...a.map((v,i)=>v-b[i]));\nconst shift=(a,b)=>Math.abs((a-b+540)%360-180);\nconst pairs=(colors,v,q)=>{let rows=[];let labs=colors.map(b=>lab(b,v,q));for(let j=1;j<labs.length;j++) for(let i=0;i<j;i++) rows.push({i:i+1,j:j+1,v,d:diff(labs[i],labs[j])});return rows.sort((a,b)=>a.d-b.d)};\nconst fill=(fg,bg,a,model)=>{let alpha=model?(model==='floor'?Math.floor:Math.round)(255*a)/255:a;return fg.map((v,c)=>{let x=alpha*v+(1-alpha)*bg[c];return model?Math.round(x):x})};\nconst nb=(bs,v='N')=>bs.slice(1).map((p,i)=>diff(lab(p,v),lab(bs[i],v)));\nconst samples=b=>[-1,0,1].flatMap(i=>[-1,0,1].flatMap(j=>[-1,0,1].map(k=>lab(b.map((v,c)=>Math.min(255,Math.max(0,v+[i,j,k][c])))))));\nconst box=(a,b)=>{const bs=samples(b);return Math.min(...samples(a).flatMap(x=>bs.map(y=>diff(x,y))))};\nconst idxmin=a=>a.indexOf(Math.min(...a));\nconst p=(a,n=6)=>Array.isArray(a)?a.map(x=>p(x,n)).join(' '):a.toFixed(n);\nconst testPalette=(cs,s,other)=>{\n const b=cs.map(rgb),bg=rgb(s.background),errors=[];\n const test=(key,x,threshold)=>{if(x<threshold)errors.push([key,x])};test('contrast',Math.min(...b.map(c=>cr(c,bg))),3);\n for(const v of vis) for(const n of [4,8]) for(const q of [false,true]){let d=pairs(b.slice(0,n),v,q)[0];test('p'+n+v+(q?'b':'a')+' '+d.i+d.j,d.d,n===4?.14:.10)}\n test('hue',30-Math.max(...b.map((c,i)=>shift(polar(c)[2],polar(rgb(other.series[i]))[2]))),0);\n for(const tk of ['focus','reference','muted'])test(tk,Math.min(...b.map(c=>diff(lab(c),lab(rgb(s[tk]))))),.10);\n for(const a of [.25,.8])for(const m of [false,'round','floor'])test('fill '+a+' '+m,Math.min(...nb(b.map(c=>fill(c,bg,a,m)))),.06);\n return errors;\n};\nfor(const [name,s] of Object.entries(schemes)){\n const other=name==='light'?dar